test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:985:                  "outcome": "The durable preservation and later safe restoration of an active or waiting agent position without treating session continuity as canonical work state.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:986:                  "details": "ID: M.1.2.1.3\nCross-cuts: M.1.2.1.1, M.1.2.1.2, M.2.5\nStatus: Accepted\nLevel 5 status: M.1.2.1.3.1–M.1.2.1.3.2 Accepted\nDecomposition review: Complete — both Level 5 concepts addressed\nDecomposition: Complete\n\nThe durable preservation and later safe restoration of an active or waiting agent position without treating session continuity as canonical work state.\n\nDECOMPOSITION — ACCEPTED\n\nAgent continuation\n  ├─ Continuation checkpoint — ADDRESSED\n  │    durable agent position and unfinished intent\n  └─ Restoration reconciliation — ADDRESSED\n       compare checkpoint with current authoritative graph\n\nCheckpoint capture and later reconciliation have different triggers. The graph remains authoritative for work; continuation records only where this agent stopped and what it intended to do next.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:997:                      "details": "ID: M.1.2.1.3.1\nCross-cuts: M.1.2.1.1, M.1.2.1.2, M.2.4, M.2.5\nStatus: Accepted\nShaping focus: Addressed\nDecomposition: Complete; natural endpoint\n\nA durable agent-specific reference to the exact Manifest position, agreed-state version, active or waiting condition, and unfinished intent needed to attempt a later continuation without copying the referenced Work System graph.\n\nBEHAVIOUR — ACCEPTED\n• Requires one valid binding and records stable checkpoint and continuation identities, agent identity, Manifest space, Objective, optional target, and exact agreed-state version.\n• Condition is Active or Waiting. Active records unfinished intent and next safe action; Waiting also records the exact response, decision, event, or condition required.\n• References canonical graph identities and carries only minimal agent-owned intent; it never copies work facts.\n• Each independent session thread has its own continuation identity and at most one current checkpoint, so concurrent sessions cannot overwrite one another.\n• Material binding, intent, next-action, or waiting-condition change durably replaces the checkpoint before preservation is claimed; retry is idempotent.\n• Replacement retains predecessor history and never creates two current checkpoints for one continuation.\n• Clearing is a deliberate recorded end; crash, timeout, silence, age, or graph change cannot silently clear it.\n• Capture, replacement, and clearing never mutate Work System state.\n• The whole update validates and persists atomically; failure leaves the prior checkpoint current and reports that the new position was not saved.\n\nENDPOINT\nPosition, condition, intent, concurrency identity, replacement, and clearing are fields and lifecycle rules of one durable checkpoint, not child concepts.\n\nCURRENT DISPOSITION — DISCONTINUED\nRemoved by W4-R4: the graph resumes work; Continuity may carry only a disposable attention handoff.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:1003:                        "reason": "Removed by W4-R4: the graph resumes work; Continuity may carry only a disposable attention handoff."
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:1011:                      "details": "ID: M.1.2.1.3.2\nCross-cuts: M.1.2.1.1, M.1.2.1.2, M.2.5.1.3, M.2.5.1.4\nStatus: Accepted\nShaping focus: Addressed\nDecomposition: Complete; natural endpoint\n\nCompares one durable Continuation checkpoint with current authoritative Manifest state before a fresh binding and projection are established.\n\nBEHAVIOUR — ACCEPTED\n• Starts from one named continuation. Missing or ambiguous candidates leave Rowan unbound with exact choices.\n• Restores and verifies current canonical Work System state before comparison; no cache, view, conversation, or checkpoint copy substitutes for it.\n• An unchanged valid checkpoint produces a fresh binding and projection at the checkpoint version.\n• An advanced graph can refresh automatically only when work and lineage still exist and no change invalidates unfinished intent, waiting condition, or next safe action; relevant differences are explained.\n• Completion, discontinuation, changed scope or authority, a materially changed blocker, a satisfied or invalid waiting condition, missing identity, invalid checkpoint, divergent heads, or any material uncertainty requires explicit resolution and leaves Rowan unbound.\n• Historical inquiry may be chosen deliberately, but former execution never resumes automatically.\n• Success creates fresh runtime objects and never mutates Work System state or silently rebases unfinished reasoning.\n• The continuation identity remains stable; its checkpoint advances only after the restored position is durable and retains its predecessor.\n• Failure leaves the original checkpoint intact and the runtime unbound; retry is idempotent.\n\nENDPOINT\nCandidate resolution, current-state restoration, version comparison, outcome classification, fresh orientation, and checkpoint advancement are stages of one restoration behaviour, not child concepts.\n\nCURRENT DISPOSITION — DISCONTINUED\nRemoved by W4-R4: a later session reloads the current repository Objective and optionally supplies Current Task.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:1371:                  "details": "ID: M.2.1.3.4\nCross-cuts: M.1.2.1.2, M.1.2.3, M.2.4.1, M.2.6.1, M.2.6.2\nStatus: Accepted\nShaping focus: Addressed\nLevel 5 status: M.2.1.3.4.1–M.2.1.3.4.4 Accepted\nDecomposition review: Complete — all four Level 5 dimensions addressed\nDecomposition: Complete\n\nAn explainable account of whether a Task is sufficiently understood and supported to begin, what uncertainty remains, and what must resolve before blocking uncertainty is cleared.\n\nREADINESS MODEL — ACCEPTED\nEach dimension is Known, Accepted assumption, or Unresolved, with rationale and evidence. Every unresolved item says whether it blocks execution and what resolves it. Overall readiness is derived as Ready, Conditional, or Not ready. Material resolution work becomes its own research, prototype, or decision Task.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:1666:                  "details": "ID: M.2.4.4.1\nStatus: Accepted\nCross-cuts: M.2.1.1, M.2.4.1, M.2.4.2\nReview pass: Addressed\nDecomposition: Complete; natural endpoint.\n\nThe deliberate assignment of an Available work item as the Objective's Current focus.\n\nBEHAVIOUR\nSelection is explicit and scoped to one Objective. The item must belong within that Objective and be Available. The Objective must have no Current item; an existing Current must first be completed or released. Selection makes the item Current and removes it from Available. If it was Next, that designation clears. Selection does not begin execution; Work commencement records that separately.\n\nENDPOINT RATIONALE\nThese are the preconditions and effects of one atomic transition. They reveal no further Manifest concept.\n\nCURRENT DISPOSITION — DISCONTINUED\nRemoved by W1.4-R1: Current is the active session Task projected onto the graph.",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:1752:          "title": "Durable work continuity",
test/fixtures/manifest-rings-view.json:2159:              "details": "ID: M.2.7.1\nStatus: Accepted\nCross-cuts: M.2.2.1, M.2.5.1, M.2.5.1.4.3.3, M.2.6.1, M.2.6.2, M.2.6.3, M.2.6.5, M.2.7.2, M.2.7.3.2\nReview pass: Addressed\nDecomposition: Complete; natural endpoint.\n\nEvery Objective, Workstream, and Task retains one permanent identity as it evolves.\n\nBEHAVIOUR — ACCEPTED\n\nAccepted creation of Work W\n          ↓ assign atomically\nOne permanent identity I\n          ↓ remains I through\nrevision · movement · focus · interruption · return · completion · discontinuation · restoration · divergent candidates\n\nDistinct work from W → new identity + provenance\nSame-work reconciliation → retain I\nDistinct-source convergence → new identity linked to every source\n\n• Creation assigns exactly one identity; failure exposes none and retry is idempotent.\n• Identity is immutable, never reassigned, and never reused after any ending.\n• Name, content, parent, position, ordering, focus, availability, version, and lifecycle state do not define identity.\n• Equal names, content, or positions never establish identity by inference.\n• Every version and lifecycle record refers to the identity it concerns.\n• Copies, split results, branches, materially different outcomes, and other distinct work receive new linked identities.\n• One identity never becomes several; several existing identities never collapse into one.\n• Convergence creates a new identity while every source identity and history remains intact.\n• Completed and discontinued identities remain addressable; renewed intent creates new linked work.\n• Identity, work, lineage, and provenance validate and preserve atomically; collisions and stale claims fail or route through divergence handling.\n\nACCEPTED ANCHOR\nOrdinary changes and same-work reconciliation retain identity. Distinct work receives a new identity. Converging distinct identities creates one new identity linked to every preserved source.\n\nENDPOINT\nComplete here. Assignment, immutability, continuity, non-reuse, and transformation rules form one identity invariant; lineage and provenance are separate concepts.\n\nCURRENT IMPLEMENTATION MEANING\nThe retained capability is implemented through the reduced Git-backed Objective graph. Retired lifecycle, versioning, proposal, and governance mechanisms in the accepted-definition text above are historical only; the Delivery Invariant and Recovery Review govern the current meaning.",
