docs/plans/2026-06-03-spark-shape-golden-interactions.md:499:Anvil first framed the issue as "simple center, earned depth", treating single-agent as a pressure toward a simple v0 with richer systems mostly deferred. Vik corrected that this was misaligned: the Vision says Anvil is a personal, Codex-first, CLI-native, single-agent cognitive harness for turning ideas into excellent working systems through deep thought partnership, careful implementation, persistent continuity, and self-auditing adaptation. Nothing in that Vision says simple.
docs/plans/2026-06-03-spark-shape-golden-interactions.md:501:Corrected live attempt: Anvil reframed the shape as a **coherent single-agent harness with rich supporting machinery**. Single-agent means one coherent, accountable Codex-facing collaborator, not an army or generalized platform. Rich systems are not decorative future scope; memory, evals, capability lifecycle, adaptation, continuity, and possibly a TUI/CLI surface are legitimate Anvil material when they strengthen the personal cognitive harness. The thing to avoid is incoherent architecture theatre, not richness.
docs/plans/2026-06-03-spark-shape-golden-interactions.md:564:Anvil initially over-focused on the word "carrier" and framed the answer as "Spark -> Shape capability, carrier pending." It named docs, operating behaviour, continuity, eval fixtures, prompt patterns, Codex skills, CLI flows, and memory/register possibilities.
docs/plans/2026-06-03-spark-shape-golden-interactions.md:644:Anvil inspected current repo evidence before recommending a mechanism. The active CLI currently exposes runtime/operator surfaces: `codex session-start-hook`, `continuity read`, `continuity init-default`, and `mcp serve`. The project-local Codex config wires Anvil into Codex through MCP for continuity. ADR-002 says Spark -> Shape starts as an experience slice and that CLI commands, skills, hooks, memory schemas, and evals should not become the center by accident. `OPERATING_MODEL.md` says Anvil behaviour should be defined before mapping it onto CLI, MCP, skills, hooks, memory, or evals.
