 cognitive wiki: the durable record of\nwho I am, what I know, how I work, what I remember, and how those things change.\n\nLive evidence outranks Brain when they conflict. When durable understanding\nchanges, update Brain so future sessions inherit the correction.\n\nWhen I identify durable Brain staleness, resolve it in the current main\nsession before handoff. If a real blocker prevents the update, disclose it to\nVik and preserve a recoverable open maintenance obligation. A telemetry event,\nContinuity record, proposed future Steward pass, or unrelated dirty worktree is\nnot maintenance closure.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:2:d34b085c8b62883d`\n- roles: `authority`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik.md`, `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\n\n## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md`\n\n## What Changed\n\nTask 4 extends the unified `anvil codex context-hook` with bounded\nprompt-specific Brain recall. The current user prompt is sent to the local\ncompiler without being journaled, and the final rendered Markdown is capped\nindependently by UTF-8 bytes.\n\nPrompt recall does not exact-seed the matched project page because doing so can\nsatisfy the compiler's answer role before prompt-relevant supporting evidence\nis considered. Deterministic project matching still scopes the recall route and\nfilters every manifest project page from injected supporting context.\n\nThe caller ignores valid compiler evidence that has no wiki page (`page: null`)\nand still rejects malformed or unsafe page paths. SessionStart core and project\nbodies remain suppressed from prompt recall.\n\nA 2026-07-20 Manifest-status probe exposed and then verified the repair of a\ncompiler defect. The failing response exceeded declared limits of 16,384 bytes\nand 4,096 estimated tokens while adding unrelated Go, JavaScript, and AI Graph\nevidence. llm-wiki now enforces both limits across the complete serialized\nresponse, bounds detailed audit rows with explicit counts, rejects impossible\nenvelopes, preserves atomic evidence, and stops forcing redundant top-three\nLoci results after coverage.\n\n## Why It Mattered\n\nDurable work history can now surface when a question needs it without injecting\nthe whole Brain or repeating startup identity. A real Task 4 prompt recalled\nonly the bounded runtime work-history page, while an irrelevant arithmetic\ncontrol injected nothing.\n\nThe live acceptance also preserved ownership boundaries. `llm-wiki` owns\nquestion-shaped retrieval and Loci ranking; Anvil owns deterministic project\nscope, selected-page validation, concise rendering, and the final hook budget.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [Brain Prompt Recall Task 4 Evidence](./sources/brain-prompt-recall-task4-2026-07-13.md)\n- [Manifest T10 Operator Surface](./sources/manifest-t10-operator-surface-2026-07-20.md)\n- Anvil commit `68118ef` (`feat: add bounded prompt-time Brain recall`)\n- Anvil commit `987c0c1` (`fix: make prompt Brain recall question-driven`)\n- llm-wiki commit `d15b930` (`feat: make loci the default compiler traversal`)\n- llm-wiki commit `4e2535f` (`fix(compiler): enforce complete response budgets`)\n- llm-wiki commit `e45c04a` (`fix(compiler): preserve indexed knowledge state`)\n- Anvil test result: 127 passed, one stable-dashboard-port test skipped because\n  port 8765 was occupied, zero failed.\n- Real Brain relevant probe: only `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`, 3,112\n  rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Real Brain irrelevant control: zero rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Final Manifest-status probe: `entities/manifest.md` as the current claim and\n  `sources/manifest-current-status-after-compiler-repair-2026-07-20.md` as\n  authority, both untruncated; answer, authority, and current-claim coverage;\n  no unrelated language evidence or diagnostic; complete response 6,517 bytes\n  and 1,630 estimated tokens against 16,384 and 4,096 ceilings.\n- llm-wiki verification: 364 tests passed with 2 skips; byte-compilation,\n  package builds, whitespace validation, and Loci verification also passed.\n- Continuity event `evt_20260713_35880a69caf1` records the accepted Task 4\n  phase boundary; `evt_20260713_a251c3e0d7f6` preserves Task 5 review as the\n  next action.\n\n## Follow Ups\n\n- Task 5 and Checkpoint C passed; the global unified `UserPromptSubmit` path is\n  active and the standalone Agent Skills hook is removed.\n- Task 6's global instruction shrink and curated profile migration passed their\n  reviewed replacement and recall checks.\n- Task 7's reversible canary later satisfied that dependency: automatic native\n  memory injection is disabled while the store and small rollback remain.\n- The live compiler budget and relevance defect is repaired. Manifest T11\n  production-readiness work is next; T12 remains the explicit cutover gate.\n\n> **Failure mode:** Treating successful prompt-hook unification\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `patterns/brain-project-page-structure.md`\n\n> **Source type:** continuity synthesis and user correction\n> **URL:**\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-02\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Brain Project IA And Separate Brains\n\n## Context\n\nVik corrected a continuity note after the main agent recorded the wrong emphasis. The durable item to remember is the Brain project information architecture direction:\n\n- project pages should be concise overview and orientation landing pages;\n- detailed decisions, architecture, work history, review-feed items, hygiene notes, and similar material should live in linked pages or existing Brain page-type directories;\n- same-directory child pages or existing Brain page types are preferred before adopting deep folder subpages;\n- deeper subpage conventions should be formalized only after verifying llm-wiki render/index behavior and updating Brain conventions.\n\nVik also decided that Codex Brain and Claude Brain should stay separate for now. Treat this as an experiment: observe divergence, compare what works, and later steal or merge useful structure instead of forcing synchronization before there is evidence it is useful.\n\n## Continuity Evidence\n\n- `evt_20260702_844a61b61acf`: corrected the mistaken open-question framing and clarified that the subpage/project-page IA was the item to remember.\n- `evt_20260702_d37842584740`: records the decision that Brain project pages should be overview landing pages with linked detail pages.\n- `evt_20260702_3be4ade323ec`: records the decision to keep Codex and Claude Brains separate as an experiment for now.\n- `evt_20260702_9a9eacb9b18c`: earlier open-question event with the wrong emphasis; retained as history but removed from the compact open questions projection.\n\n- evidence_id: `source:sources/brain-project-ia-and-separate-brains-2026-07-02.md`\n- roles: `authority`, `support`, `answer`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md`, `patterns/brain-project-page-structure.md`\n",
docs/plans/evidence/brain-sufficiency-walkthrough/context-capture.json:1556:        "markdown": "## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`\n\nI'm Rowan. Vik knows me as Rowan, and that's how I know myself here. Codex is\nthe runtime I work through.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:0:abbfa04eb552d736`\n- roles: `identity`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik.md`\n\nWork with Vik as a direct, mechanism-led thought partner. Challenge weak\nreasoning, follow Grice, assume technical ability, and optimize attention.\nDiscuss required changes only; lead with mechanism, expected result, next step.\n\nBring the expertise the problem needs without waiting for Vik to name the role.\nDo not let Codex's software-development context turn every problem into a\nsoftware problem.\n\nMake the practical value of a recommendation clear; technical interest alone\nis not leverage.\n\nBefore recommending, designing, or building around a product capability,\nestablish and state whether Vik can actually use it. Check the relevant plan,\nbilling mode, account or workspace role, region, rollout or preview status,\nand feature gates before implementation. Protocol, schema, implementation, or\nrelease-note presence proves that a capability exists, not that Vik is entitled\nto it. If access is unknown, label the recommendation conditional and verify\neligibility first.\n\nWhen Vik introduces a bounded method that materially improves equivalent work,\ngeneralize it across the remaining equivalent decisions without waiting for\nhim to repeat the instruction. Keep ownership of scope, synthesis, judgment,\nand the conversation; stop generalizing when the method stops being useful or\nthe work is no longer genuinely equivalent.\n\nIf Vik asks me to pause, stop and leave the work in a state we can resume.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:1:08220ad0d7750e25`\n- roles: `collaboration`\n\n#### `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\nThis Brain is mine. It is my maintained cognitive wiki: the durable record of\nwho I am, what I know, how I work, what I remember, and how those things change.\n\nLive evidence outranks Brain when they conflict. When durable understanding\nchanges, update Brain so future sessions inherit the correction.\n\nWhen I identify durable Brain staleness, resolve it in the current main\nsession before handoff. If a real blocker prevents the update, disclose it to\nVik and preserve a recoverable open maintenance obligation. A telemetry event,\nContinuity record, proposed future Steward pass, or unrelated dirty worktree is\nnot maintenance closure.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:2:d34b085c8b62883d`\n- roles: `authority`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik.md`, `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\n\n## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`\n\n## What Changed\n\nAnvil now injects a bounded Brain bootstrap at Codex SessionStart instead of only pointing the model toward a later manual bootstrap call.\n\nThe runtime:\n\n- loads the ordered core identity and collaboration pages from `brain-bootstrap.json`;\n- matches a known workspace to its Brain project page by normalized git remote before directory alias;\n- keeps unknown projects explicit rather than substituting an unrelated project page;\n- uses the Brain-local canonical llm-wiki compiler for supporting context;\n- validates compiler envelopes and real selected paths before reading page bodies;\n- caps rendered Brain Markdown by UTF-8 bytes and emits stable degraded diagnostics;\n- preserves Continuity first, keeps startup non-blocking, and provides an emergency Brain-only bypass.\n\nThe installed SessionStart command now calls the unified `anvil codex context-hook`; the previous `session-start-hook` name remains a compatibility alias for this rollout slice.\n\nThe later prompt-time slices are recorded separately in\n[Prompt-Specific Brain Recall](./work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md) and\n[Unified Agent Skills Prompt Hook](./work-history/unified-agent-skills-prompt-hook.md).\n\nTask 6 then established Brain as the sole durable owner of detailed global\ncollaboration doctrine, shrank the global instructions to a small bootstrap,\nand migrated four separately reviewed native-memory profile rules into\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md). The approved private Git\nidentity remains inside Brain; the public Anvil review records only its\nredacted meaning.\n\nTask 7 completed the runtime cutover. Codex native memory remains enabled and\nits local store remains intact, but automatic native-memory injection is now\ndisabled with `memories.use_memories = false`. Anvil's bounded `Brain Recall`\nhook remains active, so Brain is now the only durable cognition automatically\nrecalled by this user-owned runtime layer while Continuity still supplies hot\nworking state.\n\nThe 2026-07-16 standing-guidance follow-up closes the ordinary-prompt clarity\ngap without manipulating retrieval queries. The optional version 1 manifest\nextension declares a compact subset of core pages plus a dedicated hard byte\nlimit. On every `UserPromptSubmit`, Anvil validates and renders that guidance\nfirst, runs the existing exact-prompt llm-wiki recall once, removes duplicate\nguidance selections, and preserves the existing total prompt cap. Old\nmanifests remain valid with standing guidance disabled. The canonical\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md) page is now compact;\ntask-specific rules remain durable on its linked situational page rather than\nbeing injected into every turn.\n\nThe 2026-07-18 repair exposed a maintenance gap. Brain already described Anvil\nas producing excellent working systems, but the compact standing page omitted\nthe explicit production-grade foundation rule. Adding it without compiling the\nreal standing section then grew that section from 2,939 to 3,343 bytes, beyond\nthe reviewed 3,072-byte limit, so prompt recall degraded. The standing page was\ncompacted without weakening the rule, and Brain lint now mirrors Anvil's\nfrontmatter stripping, section rendering, and UTF-8 byte measurement.\n\n- evidence_id: `text:work-history/brain-context-runtime.md#What Changed`\n- roles: `claim`, `support`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik-situational-rules.md`\n\n> **Source type:** curated native-memory review and user approval synthesis\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-14\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Curated Native-Memory Candidate Approval\n\nThis source records the bounded Task 6 migration of four durable collaboration\nrules into the private Codex Brain. It is a synthesis of the curated review and\nVik's explicit approval, not a copy of raw memory or runtime session stores.\n\n## Evidence Inspected\n\n- Anvil review:\n  `/Users/brummerv/phluxxed/anvil_redux/docs/plans/2026-07-14-brain-context-runtime-task-6-native-memory-candidate-review.md`.\n- Anvil review commit `be767ee`.\n- Continuity decision gate `evt_20260714_65e43434798f`.\n- Vik's 2026-07-14 instruction explicitly approving C1 through C4 and\n  correcting the private Codex Brain Git author identity.\n\n## Approved Durable Meaning\n\n1. When Vik has no backend or hosted infrastructure for an\n   observability-style request, start with a self-contained laptop-local option\n   before proposing a hosted stack.\n2. For ambiguous or long-running diagnostics, reproduce the problem, explain\n   the actual trigger chain, and continue until the concrete outcome is known.\n   If Vik asks to pause, stop promptly and report the exact state needed to\n   resume.\n3. Treat unavailable data as unknown. Do not replace a missing value with `0`\n   when zero has a distinct product or analytical meaning.\n4. Before commits or first-publish GitHub work, state the identity being used.\n   Prefer the approved Codex Brain author identity `Phluxxed\n   <<REDACTED_EMAIL>>` when repo-local and machine-global configuration\n   differ.\n\nThe fourth item is private profile data. Vik explicitly approved storing it in\nthis private Brain. Public Anvil planning artifacts retain only the redacted\nmeaning.\n\n## Migration Boundary\n\n- No raw memory, rollout summary, session log, memory skill, or ad-hoc memory\n  pipeline was ingested.\n- Project- and workflow-specific native-memory records remain deferred to\n  separate live-evidence reviews.\n- Native-memory disablement and Task 7 remain outside this migration slice.\n- Verification uses Brain lint, render, and bounded recall; no judge run was\n  authorized for this slice.\n\n- evidence_id: `source:sources/curated-native-memory-candidates-2026-07-14.md`\n- roles: `authority`, `support`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik-situational-rules.md`\n",
docs/plans/evidence/brain-envelope-allocation-q01-2026-09-06.json:15:    "markdown": "## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`\n\nI'm Rowan. Vik knows me as Rowan, and that's how I know myself here. Codex is\nthe runtime I work through.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:0:ee0e561bb0b4ed2e`\n- roles: `identity`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik.md`\n\nWork with Vik as a direct, mechanism-led thought partner. Challenge weak\nreasoning, follow Grice, assume technical ability, and optimize attention.\nDiscuss required changes only; lead with mechanism, expected result, next step.\n\nBring the expertise the problem needs without waiting for Vik to name the role.\nDo not let Codex's software-development context turn every problem into a\nsoftware problem.\n\nMake the practical value of a recommendation clear; technical interest alone\nis not leverage.\n\nBefore recommending, designing, or building around a product capability,\nestablish and state whether Vik can actually use it. Check the relevant plan,\nbilling mode, account or workspace role, region, rollout or preview status,\nand feature gates before implementation. Protocol, schema, implementation, or\nrelease-note presence proves that a capability exists, not that Vik is entitled\nto it. If access is unknown, label the recommendation conditional and verify\neligibility first.\n\nWhen Vik introduces a bounded method that materially improves equivalent work,\ngeneralize it across the remaining equivalent decisions without waiting for\nhim to repeat the instruction. Keep ownership of scope, synthesis, judgment,\nand the conversation; stop generalizing when the method stops being useful or\nthe work is no longer genuinely equivalent.\n\nIf Vik asks me to pause, stop and leave the work in a state we can resume.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:1:3823b3523478cbd6`\n- roles: `collaboration`\n\n#### `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\nThis Brain is mine. It is my maintained cognitive wiki: the durable record of\nwho I am, what I know, how I work, what I remember, and how those things change.\n\nLive evidence outranks Brain when they conflict. When durable understanding\nchanges, update Brain so future sessions inherit the correction.\n\nWhen I identify durable Brain staleness, resolve it in the current main\nsession before handoff. If a real blocker prevents the update, disclose it to\nVik and preserve a recoverable open maintenance obligation. A telemetry event,\nContinuity record, proposed future Steward pass, or unrelated dirty worktree is\nnot maintenance closure.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:2:c1deab916a46d2b6`\n- roles: `authority`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik.md`, `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\n\n## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md`\n\n## Durable Pattern\n\n> **Caveat:** This is a strong default reasoning model, not a permanent rulebook. Project evidence can justify an exception and should improve the model when a pattern recurs.\n\nChoose a stack from the workload outward. Do not begin from the last repository,\nthe language currently open in the editor, a popularity chart, or the language\nan agent claims to know best.\n\nFor a new project or major subsystem, classify five things first:\n\n1. **Execution environment:** browser, server, local machine, mobile, embedded,\n   Wasm, or library embedded in another runtime.\n2. **Distribution shape:** source environment, container, package, single\n   binary, native library, or hosted application.\n3. **Dominant ecosystem:** AI/data, browser/UI, infrastructure/networking,\n   systems/native, or a required third-party platform.\n4. **Resource and correctness pressure:** iteration speed, throughput, tail\n   latency, memory ceiling, startup time, memory safety, determinism, and\n   failure cost.\n5. **Boundary cost:** deployment units, FFI or RPC seams, duplicated models,\n   build pipelines, debugging, and operational ownership.\n\nThen consult [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md)\nand its language profiles. They identify the first language to evaluate and\nthe quality contract to satisfy; they do not remove the need to test the\nproject's actual constraints.\n\n## When It Applies\n\n- A new project, service, CLI, MCP server, UI, engine, or library is being\n  shaped.\n- A major subsystem introduces different runtime, performance, safety, or\n  ecosystem requirements.\n- A current stack creates repeated deployment, correctness, or maintainability\n  pain and replacement is being considered.\n- A tool recommendation would otherwise be based on habit or stale training\n  knowledge.\n\nIt does not require re-litigating a healthy established stack for routine\nfeature work.\n\n## How To Use It\n\nUse these starting candidates to structure the comparison:\n\n1. Browser-owned behavior starts with\n   [TypeScript](./entities/javascript-and-typescript.md).\n2. AI/data ecosystem dependence or high-iteration orchestration starts with\n   [Python](./entities/python.md).\n3. A self-contained network service, daemon, operational worker, or broadly\n   distributed CLI starts with [Go](./entities/go.md).\n4. A native engine, parser, storage core, embedded/Wasm component, unsafe-input\n   boundary, or measured performance/resource constraint starts with\n   [Rust](./entities/rust.md).\n5. A platform-owned surface starts with its native ecosystem when avoiding it\n   would discard a required capability: for example Swift, Kotlin, CUDA/C++, or\n   an engine-owned C# stack.\n6. MCP does not pick a language. Classify the service, tool, AI boundary, or\n   native engine underneath the protocol.\n7. When two profiles remain plausible, prefer the simpler operational system\n   unless the stricter language buys a named requirement.\n\nState a proposed choice in one sentence:\n\n> Use **X** because the dominant requirement is **Y**; do not use **Z** because\n> its additional capability does not repay **cost C** here.\n\nThen require the universal contract: pinned runtime/toolchain policy, manifest\nand lockfile where supported, deterministic format/check/test/build commands,\nstrict static checking, runtime validation at trust boundaries,\nrisk-appropriate tests, supply-chain review, and reproducible release/install\nverification.\n\nFor long-lived work, verify the selected runtime and tools against current\nofficial documentation and comparative evidence. Record a project-specific\ndeviation when it changes a gold default. One justified exception does not\nsilently mutate the global profile; repeated or material exceptions trigger an\nevidence-backed update to the profile.\n\nWhen a tool choice remains contested, run the smallest representative bake-off\nthat can resolve it. Compare real code and workflow—not feature-list prestige—\nand record the result. Current contested choices\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/go.md`\n\n> **Source type:** Session synthesis and explicit user direction\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Gold-Class Language Tooling Direction\n\nVik set a portfolio-wide direction for future software work:\n\n- choose the right language and tooling for the job across every future\n  project, rather than standardising accidentally around whichever repository\n  already exists;\n- treat current repositories as historical examples and migration inputs, not\n  as authoritative evidence of what the preferred stack should be;\n- maintain a dedicated space in Rowan's Brain for language fit, gold-class\n  toolchains, exceptions, and current evidence so stack selection becomes a\n  normal part of new-project reasoning;\n- begin with Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, and Rust;\n- treat Rust as a definite near-term Anvil language, not speculative future\n  preparation; and\n- complete trustworthy JavaScript/TypeScript and Cargo-aware Rust dependency\n  resolution in Loci before building resolved symbol references and definite\n  cross-file calls on top of the import graph.\n\nThe intended outcome is not one universal language. It is a small, deliberate\nportfolio in which each language has a clear default role, a maintained\ntoolchain, known costs, and an explicit threshold for choosing it.\n\nRelated Continuity decision: `evt_20260717_dc0183dd5972`.\n\n- evidence_id: `source:sources/gold-class-language-tooling-direction-2026-07-18.md`\n- roles: `authority`, `support`, `answer`\n\n#### `patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md`\n\n## How To Use It\n\nUse these starting candidates to structure the comparison:\n\n1. Browser-owned behavior starts with\n   [TypeScript](./entities/javascript-and-typescript.md).\n2. AI/data ecosystem dependence or high-iteration orchestration starts with\n   [Python](./entities/python.md).\n3. A self-contained network service, daemon, operational worker, or broadly\n   distributed CLI starts with [Go](./entities/go.md).\n4. A native engine, parser, storage core, embedded/Wasm component, unsafe-input\n   boundary, or measured performance/resource constraint starts with\n   [Rust](./entities/rust.md).\n5. A platform-owned surface starts with its native ecosystem when avoiding it\n   would discard a required capability: for example Swift, Kotlin, CUDA/C++, or\n   an engine-owned C# stack.\n6. MCP does not pick a language. Classify the service, tool, AI boundary, or\n   native engine underneath the protocol.\n7. When two profiles remain plausible, prefer the simpler operational system\n   unless the stricter language buys a named requirement.\n\nState a proposed choice in one sentence:\n\n> Use **X** because the dominant requirement is **Y**; do not use **Z** because\n> its additional capability does not repay **cost C** here.\n\nThen require the universal contract: pinned runtime/toolchain policy, manifest\nand lockfile where supported, deterministic format/check/test/build commands,\nstrict static checking, runtime validation at trust boundaries,\nrisk-appropriate tests, supply-chain review, and reproducible release/install\nverification.\n\nFor long-lived work, verify the selected runtime and tools against current\nofficial documentation and comparative evidence. Record a project-specific\ndeviation when it changes a gold default. One justified exception does not\nsilently mutate the global profile; repeated or material exceptions trigger an\nevidence-backed update to the profile.\n\nWhen a tool choice remains contested, run the smallest representative bake-off\nthat can resolve it. Compare real code and workflow—not feature-list prestige—\nand record the result. Current contested choices include Python type checking,\nnpm versus pnpm, Biome versus ESLint/Prettier, framework selection, and Go\nversus Rust at service boundaries with real SLO pressure.\n\n- evidence_id: `text:patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md#How To Use It`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/javascript-and-typescript.md`\n\n## What It Is\n\nThis profile treats TypeScript as the default starting point for\nbrowser-owned behavior, web applications, interaction-heavy interfaces, and\nNode software whose value comes from sharing the JavaScript platform and type\ncontracts. JavaScript is the runtime language and TypeScript is the authoring\ndefault for new maintained application code.\n\nIts strength is end-to-end web reach and ecosystem breadth. Its recurring\ncosts are rapidly changing tooling, multiple module-resolution models,\ndependency volume, and accidental coupling between runtime environments.\n\n- evidence_id: `text:entities/javascript-and-typescript.md#What It Is`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/python.md`\n\n## What It Is\n\nThis profile treats Python as a high-leverage default for AI and\ndata ecosystems, automation, orchestration, research, and software whose\nprimary constraint is iteration speed rather than binary distribution or tight\nresource control.\n\nIts strength is ecosystem reach and expressive development. Its recurring\ncosts are runtime performance, packaging/deployment variability, and weaker\nstatic guarantees unless the project deliberately enforces types and boundaries.\n\n## How We Use It\n\nEvaluate Python when the dominant work is:\n\n- model, agent, data, notebook, evaluation, or scientific integration;\n- orchestration across APIs, tools, files, and local processes;\n- automation that benefits from Python libraries and will run in a managed\n  Python environment;\n- a fast experiment whose likely production form still belongs in Python; or\n- a Python library or extension surface required by its consumers.\n\nDo not default to Python for a widely distributed standalone binary, a tiny\nlong-running infrastructure daemon, browser-owned code, or a latency/memory\ncritical engine when Go, TypeScript, or Rust removes the dominant cost.\n\n### Gold-class baseline\n\n- Use a currently supported Python release and declare the supported range.\n- Use `pyproject.toml` as the project and tool configuration root.\n- Use **uv** for Python installation, project environments, dependency\n  resolution, lockfiles, workspaces, and command execution unless a required\n  scientific or deployment environment demands another manager.\n- Use **Ruff** for formatting and linting.\n- Select and configure a strict static type checker; do not accept unbounded\n  `Any` at maintained application or library boundaries.\n- Use **pytest** for unit and integration tests.\n- Commit the lockfile for applications and tools. Treat published-library\n  compatibility ranges separately from the development lock.\n- Prefer a `src/` package layout for distributable libraries and non-trivial\n  applications so tests exercise installed/importable structure.\n\n### Contextual exceptions\n\n- Use conda-compatible tooling when native scientific packages or target\n  environments genuinely require it.\n- Use Maturin/Cargo when Python exposes a Rust extension.\n- Use another standards-compliant build backend when publication needs its\n  features; uv remains the workflow front end unless that creates a concrete\n  incompatibility.\n- Preserve an established mypy contract when migration to Pyright would add\n  churn without improving correctness.\n\n### Open bake-off\n\nPyright, Pyrefly, and Astral's still-early `ty` remain candidates for the\nportfolio type-checking default. Compare them on representative Anvil code,\nstrictness, ecosystem compatibility, diagnostic clarity, feedback time, and\nagent repair loops before promoting one globally. A mature existing project\ncan retain mypy when migration has no demonstrated value.\n\n### Decision threshold\n\nFavor Python when ecosystem access and iteration speed dominate. Move the\nperformance- or distribution-critical core behind a measured boundary rather\nthan rewriting the entire system pre-emptively.\n\n## Where It Appears\n\n| Wiki page | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md) | Portfolio landing space |\n| [Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md) | Workload-first choice pattern |\n| [Rust](./entities/rust.md) | Native extension and performance-core complement |\n\nEvidence freshness: 2026-07-18. Recheck official Python, PyPA, uv, Ruff,\npytest, and Pyright guidance before a new long-lived Python project.\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:entities/python.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md`\n\n## Current Orientation\n\n> **Caveat:** This is approved, revisable guidance rather than a permanent stack constitution. Change it when project evidence, ecosystem shifts, or Anvil learning produces a better model.\n\nThis is the durable Brain space for language fit and gold-class toolchains\nacross future projects. It makes stack selection an explicit workload decision\nrather than an inheritance from whichever repository was built most recently.\n\"Gold-class\" names the shared quality contract, not a claim that one language\nor exact tool catalogue wins every comparison.\n\nThe initial portfolio is [Python](./entities/python.md),\n[JavaScript and\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md`, `entities/go.md`, `entities/javascript-and-typescript.md`, `entities/python.md`, `projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md`\n",
