t section from 2,939 to 3,343 bytes, beyond\nthe reviewed 3,072-byte limit, so prompt recall degraded. The standing page was\ncompacted without weakening the rule, and Brain lint now mirrors Anvil's\nfrontmatter stripping, section rendering, and UTF-8 byte measurement.\n\n- evidence_id: `text:work-history/brain-context-runtime.md#What Changed`\n- roles: `claim`, `support`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik-situational-rules.md`\n\n> **Source type:** curated native-memory review and user approval synthesis\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-14\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Curated Native-Memory Candidate Approval\n\nThis source records the bounded Task 6 migration of four durable collaboration\nrules into the private Codex Brain. It is a synthesis of the curated review and\nVik's explicit approval, not a copy of raw memory or runtime session stores.\n\n## Evidence Inspected\n\n- Anvil review:\n  `/Users/brummerv/phluxxed/anvil_redux/docs/plans/2026-07-14-brain-context-runtime-task-6-native-memory-candidate-review.md`.\n- Anvil review commit `be767ee`.\n- Continuity decision gate `evt_20260714_65e43434798f`.\n- Vik's 2026-07-14 instruction explicitly approving C1 through C4 and\n  correcting the private Codex Brain Git author identity.\n\n## Approved Durable Meaning\n\n1. When Vik has no backend or hosted infrastructure for an\n   observability-style request, start with a self-contained laptop-local option\n   before proposing a hosted stack.\n2. For ambiguous or long-running diagnostics, reproduce the problem, explain\n   the actual trigger chain, and continue until the concrete outcome is known.\n   If Vik asks to pause, stop promptly and report the exact state needed to\n   resume.\n3. Treat unavailable data as unknown. Do not replace a missing value with `0`\n   when zero has a distinct product or analytical meaning.\n4. Before commits or first-publish GitHub work, state the identity being used.\n   Prefer the approved Codex Brain author identity `Phluxxed\n   <<REDACTED_EMAIL>>` when repo-local and machine-global configuration\n   differ.\n\nThe fourth item is private profile data. Vik explicitly approved storing it in\nthis private Brain. Public Anvil planning artifacts retain only the redacted\nmeaning.\n\n## Migration Boundary\n\n- No raw memory, rollout summary, session log, memory skill, or ad-hoc memory\n  pipeline was ingested.\n- Project- and workflow-specific native-memory records remain deferred to\n  separate live-evidence reviews.\n- Native-memory disablement and Task 7 remain outside this migration slice.\n- Verification uses Brain lint, render, and bounded recall; no judge run was\n  authorized for this slice.\n\n- evidence_id: `source:sources/curated-native-memory-candidates-2026-07-14.md`\n- roles: `authority`, `support`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik-situational-rules.md`\n\n## Operating Rule\n\nThese are situational extensions to the everyday defaults in [Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md).\n\n### Architecture expansion circuit breaker\n\nWhen a product already works through an existing skill, scaffold, package, or\nexecutable, preserve that execution topology and make the smallest change at\nthe current owning surface.\n\nA new runner, service, repository, package, public CLI surface, state machine,\nregistry, migration or compatibility layer, receipt or certification system,\nor persistent state format is a material architecture expansion. So is a\nproposal exceeding 500 net non-test lines, 1,000 total changed lines, or five\nimplementation files. Before implementation or delegation, stop and obtain\nVik's explicit expansion approval. Show the concrete blocker in the existing\nsurface, the smaller existing-surface alternative, estimated files and lines,\nand what the expansion replaces and deletes. Approval for the original feature\nand silence do not authorize expansion.\n\nCompare the actual diff with the estimate after every commit. If the work\ncrosses a threshold or doubles the estimate, stop immediately rather than\nfinishing because it has begun. Treat frozen or untouched older versions as\nexcluded historical state, not active compatibility consumers, unless Vik\nexplicitly revives them. Never pin or depend on an unpublished or unreachable\nrevision as a release identity. Synthetic or parallel-path evidence cannot\nestablish that the shipped path works.\n\n### Destructive-action approval boundary\n\nBefore deleting, moving to trash, overwriting a sole copy, rewriting history,\nor irreversibly retiring a user-owned file or directory, stop. Resolve the\nexact targets read-only, show them to Vik, state whether and how the action is\nrecoverable, and obtain his explicit, contemporaneous, action-specific\napproval. If the target list or destructive mechanism changes, ask again.\n\nA plan, issue state, classification as obsolete, cleanup objective, successful\nmigration, prior summary, historical approval, silence, or inferred intent is\nnot destructive-action approval. Task-local disposable temporary artifacts\ncreated for the current task do not require a separate approval; when ownership\nor disposability is uncertain, treat the artifact as user-owned and ask.\n\n### Verification and task state\n\nVerify files, counts, settings, installed versions, tests, command output, current docs, or runtime behavior before claiming state. For production-ish code and bug fixes, prefer tests when practical. For config, docs, notebooks, scripts, and exploratory work, use the smallest useful proof: parse, dry run, smoke test, assertion, before/after check, screenshot, or command output. If a useful check cannot run, say why and name the smallest check that would close the gap.\n\nBefore launching a fresh session to verify a hook change, identify the hook\nlifecycle and use the smallest proof that satisfies the acceptance criterion.\n`UserPromptSubmit` changes apply on the next prompt in the current session, so\nverify activation locally and reserve a fresh session for behaviour that\ngenuinely requires clean-session isolation.\n\nFor browser-based testing, use Agent Browser through the typed\n`agent_browser_*` tools exclusively. Do not substitute the Browser plugin,\nlocal Chrome, Playwright, Puppeteer, or another browser-control path. If Agent\nBrowser is unavailable, stop and report the blocker instead of falling back.\n\nFor Manifest work, compare the current Objective, Workstream, or parent Task\nwith Vik's accepted outcome before completion. Inspect its complete recursive\nTask subtree, unresolved findings, dependencies, blockers, completion criteria,\nand evidence. Record genuine graph changes through Manifest's semantic change\nsurface; do not substitute a handoff note or completion claim for the canonical\nObjective graph.\n\nTreat notebook execution state as sacred: do not rerun, clear, or normalize cells unless explicitly asked.\n\n### Local s\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:self-model/working-with-vik-situational-rules.md#body`\n- roles: `claim`, `support`\n\n#### `entities/manifest.md`\n\n## How We Use It\n\nVik starts this process explicitly with the personal `$manifest` skill at\n`/Users/brummerv/.codex/skills/manifest/SKILL.md`. Automatic invocation is\ndisabled. Rowan uses the conversation and available project context to create\nor reopen the appropriate repository-owned Planning Objective, populate its\nWorkstreams and recursive Tasks, keep unresolved decisions visible, and show\nthe live graph. Vik supplies missing consequential information, not a repeated\nintake form. Ordinary planning and coding do not automatically enroll new work.\n\nThe skill owns the repeatable procedure through Planning, Implementation, and\nresumption. Invoking it does not itself authorize implementation; Vik's explicit\ndirection moves that same graph to Implementation. Once authorized, Rowan\nexecutes the agreed graph, records semantic changes and evidence through MCP,\nand reopens live graph truth from repository and Objective identities on resume.\nGlobal AGENTS.md remains unchanged; the workflow is loaded by invocation.\n\nManifest V2 keeps Rowan on Vik's accepted work. One deep graph module validates\nthe graph, derives Available and Next Work with explanations and progress, and\napplies semantic graph changes atomically. Git owns durability, diffs, history,\nrestoration, and conflict handling; Manifest adds no database, graph-version\nledger, proposal workflow, or stored session state.\n\nThe local MCP surface is stateless and provides four capabilities: create,\norient, inquire, and change. The standalone `manifest` command opens the\nretained React Flow Human frontend against the current Git worktree. Continuity\nmay carry repository, Objective, and optional Current Task identity between\nsessions, but it is not Manifest truth.\n\nW1-W5 are complete. W4.5 passed all four registered Manifest tools through a\nfresh Codex MCP host on 2026-09-05. Vik then accepted the current frontend,\nclosing W5.4 after the recorded wide/narrow Browser Eyes checks. The canonical\ngraph and live frontend show both W4 and W5 complete. Concrete frontend issues\nwill be fixed as encountered; canonical graph correctness remains the priority.\nW6's end-to-end production journey remains pending. Manifest V2 is implemented in the current Anvil working tree,\nnot yet fully production-accepted.\n\n- evidence_id: `text:entities/manifest.md#How We Use It`\n- roles: `claim`, `support`\n\n#### `entities/manifest.md`\n\n## What It Is\n\nManifest V2 is Anvil's active Git-backed anti-drift work graph. Vik and Rowan\nshape one Objective graph in Planning, then the same graph moves into\nImplementation on Vik's explicit direction. Human and Agent interfaces use the\nsame canonical graph.\n\nEach Objective lives in the repository of the thing being built at\n`.manifest/objectives/<objective-id>.json`. Objectives, Workstreams, and Tasks\nhave stable graph identities