t to Python for a widely distributed standalone binary, a tiny\nlong-running infrastructure daemon, browser-owned code, or a latency/memory\ncritical engine when Go, TypeScript, or Rust removes the dominant cost.\n\n### Gold-class baseline\n\n- Use a currently supported Python release and declare the supported range.\n- Use `pyproject.toml` as the project and tool configuration root.\n- Use **uv** for Python installation, project environments, dependency\n  resolution, lockfiles, workspaces, and command execution unless a required\n  scientific or deployment environment demands another manager.\n- Use **Ruff** for formatting and linting.\n- Select and configure a strict static type checker; do not accept unbounded\n  `Any` at maintained application or library boundaries.\n- Use **pytest** for unit and integration tests.\n- Commit the lockfile for applications and tools. Treat published-library\n  compatibility ranges separately from the development lock.\n- Prefer a `src/` package layout for distributable libraries and non-trivial\n  applications so tests exercise installed/importable structure.\n\n### Contextual exceptions\n\n- Use conda-compatible tooling when native scientific packages or target\n  environments genuinely require it.\n- Use Maturin/Cargo when Python exposes a Rust extension.\n- Use another standards-compliant build backend when publication needs its\n  features; uv remains the workflow front end unless that creates a concrete\n  incompatibility.\n- Preserve an established mypy contract when migration to Pyright would add\n  churn without improving correctness.\n\n### Open bake-off\n\nPyright, Pyrefly, and Astral's still-early `ty` remain candidates for the\nportfolio type-checking default. Compare them on representative Anvil code,\nstrictness, ecosystem compatibility, diagnostic clarity, feedback time, and\nagent repair loops before promoting one globally. A mature existing project\ncan retain mypy when migration has no demonstrated value.\n\n### Decision threshold\n\nFavor Python when ecosystem access and iteration speed dominate. Move the\nperformance- or distribution-critical core behind a measured boundary rather\nthan rewriting the entire system pre-emptively.\n\n## Where It Appears\n\n| Wiki page | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md) | Portfolio landing space |\n| [Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md) | Workload-first choice pattern |\n| [Rust](./entities/rust.md) | Native extension and performance-core complement |\n\nEvidence freshness: 2026-07-18. Recheck official Python, PyPA, uv, Ruff,\npytest, and Pyright guidance before a new long-lived Python project.\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:entities/python.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/manifest.md`\n\n{\"authority\":\"target_wiki_steward\",\"claim_key\":\"temporal-claim:sha256:430934d45aa188d183479bff6e16dc235fbc219de868aa2a503483f1903c71cd\",\"decision\":\"supersede\",\"object\":{\"datatype\":\"type:text\",\"kind\":\"literal\",\"value\":\"On 6 September 2026 Vik identified seven known Implementation additions missed by Manifest's Git-only marker: W2.2, W2.4, W2.5 and its three children, and the W2.6 parent. They were bundled with earlier Work in the first Objective commit; first Git appearance was not their creation time. Vik approved correcting this coverage. The primary recorded an evidence-linked standalone Creation phase: Implementation. note in each existing Task's details through MCP. The Human view now honours exact Planning or Implementation creation notes before Git inference, ignores incidental and fenced examples, treats conflicting notes as unknown, and reads current details separately from the Git cache. All ten known Brain additions, including the three already Git-proved W2.6 children, now show the same marker; unrelated unknowns stay unknown. The real-graph regression and 1440x1000/430x932 browser checks pass without changing execution facts, selection or phase-switch geometry. No schema field, separate origin store or baseline file was added. The earlier Git-only completion claim was too narrow.\"},\"predicate\":\"manifest:task_creation_marker\",\"recorded_at\":\"2026-09-06T08:17:00Z\",\"revision_id\":\"temporal-revision:sha256:74a520bbca833ff2e12648f64de150d0734ebc4687af3d468c618ab39387f20d\",\"status\":\"settled\",\"steward_evidence_refs\":[\"sources/manifest-known-creation-history-correction-2026-09-06.md\"],\"subject\":{\"kind\":\"resolved_page\",\"page\":\"entities/manifest.md\"},\"view\":\"current\",\"world_validity\":{\"from\":{\"kind\":\"known\",\"value\":\"2026-09-06\"},\"to\":{\"kind\":\"open\"}}}\n\n- evidence_id: `temporal:entities/manifest.md#temporal-revision:sha256:74a520bbca833ff2e12648f64de150d0734ebc4687af3d468c618ab39387f20d`\n- roles: `answer`, `current_claim`, `authority`\n\n#### `patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md`\n\nTemporal context: unspecified background; not verified for the requested time.\n\n## Durable Pattern\n\n> **Caveat:** This is a strong default reasoning model, not a permanent rulebook. Project evidence can justify an exception and should improve the model when a pattern recurs.\n\nChoose a stack from the workload outward. Do not begin from the last repository,\nthe language currently open in the editor, a popularity chart, or the language\nan agent claims to know best.\n\nFor a new project or major subsystem, classify five things first:\n\n1. **Execution environment:** browser, server, local machine, mobile, embedded,\n   Wasm, or library embedded in another runtime.\n2. **Distribution shape:** source environment, container, package, single\n   binary, native library, or hosted application.\n3. **Dominant ecosystem:** AI/data, browser/UI, infrastructure/networking,\n   systems/native, or a required third-party platform.\n4. **Resource and correctness pressure:** iteration speed, throughput, tail\n   latency, memory ceiling, startup time, memory safety, determinism, and\n   failure cost.\n5. **Boundary cost:** deployment units, FFI or RPC seams, duplicated models,\n   build pipelines, debugging, and operational ownership.\n\nThen consult [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md)\nand its language profiles. They identify the first language to evaluate and\nthe quality contract to satisfy; they do not remove the need to test the\nproject's actual constraints.\n\n## When It Applies\n\n- A new project, service, CLI, MCP server, UI, engine, or library is being\n  shaped.\n- A major subsystem introduces different runtime, performance, safety, or\n  ecosystem requirements.\n- A current stack creates repeated deployment, correctness, or maintainability\n  pain and replacement is being considered.\n- A tool recommendation would otherwise be based on habit or stale training\n  knowledge.\n\nIt does not require re-litigating a healthy established stack for routine\nfeature work.\n\n## How To Use It\n\nUse these starting candidates to structure the comparison:\n\n1. Browser-owned behavior starts with\n   [TypeScript](./entities/javascript-and-typescript.md).\n2. AI/data ecosystem dependence or high-iteration orchestration starts with\n   [Python](./entities/python.md).\n3. A self-contained network service, daemon, operational worker, or broadly\n   distributed CLI starts with [Go](./entities/go.md).\n4. A native engine, parser, storage core, embedded/Wasm component, unsafe-input\n   boundary, or measured performance/resource constraint starts with\n   [Rust](./entities/rust.md).\n5. A platform-owned surface starts with its native ecosystem when avoiding it\n   would discard a required capability: for example Swift, Kotlin, CUDA/C++, or\n   an engine-owned C# stack.\n6. MCP does not pick a language. Classify the service, tool, AI boundary, or\n   native engine underneath the protocol.\n7. When two profiles remain plausible, prefer the simpler operational system\n   unless the stricter language buys a named requirement.\n\nState a proposed choice in one sentence:\n\n> Use **X** because the dominant requirement is **Y**; do not use **Z** because\n> its additional capability does not repay **cost C** here.\n\nThen require the universal contract: pinned runtime/toolchain policy, manifest\nand lockfile where supported, deterministic format/check/test/build commands,\nstrict static checking, runtime validation at trust boundaries,\nrisk-appropriate tests, supply-chain review, and reproducible release/install\nverification.\n\nFor long-lived work, verify the selected runtime and tools against current\nofficial documentation and comparative evidence. Record a project-specific\ndeviation when it changes a gold default. One justified exception does not\nsilently mutate the global profile; repeated or material exceptions trigger an\nevidence-backed update to the profile.\n\nWhen a tool choice remains contested, run the smallest representative bake-off\nthat can resolve it. Compare real code and workflow—not feature-list prestige—\nand record the result. Current contested choices\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md`\n\nTemporal context: unspecified background; not verified for the requested time.\n\n## Current Orientation\n\n> **Caveat:** This is approved, revisable guidance rather than a permanent stack constitution. Change it when project evidence, ecosystem shifts, or Anvil learning produces a better model.\n\nThis is the durable Brain space for language fit and gold-class toolchains\nacross future projects. It makes stack selection an explicit workload decision\nrather than an inheritance from whichever repository was built most recently.\n\"Gold-class\" names the shared quality contract, not a claim that one language\nor exact tool catalogue wins every comparison.\n\nThe initial portfolio is [Python](./entities/python.md),\n[JavaScript and TypeScript](./entities/javascript-and-typescript.md),\n[Go](./entities/go.md), and [Rust](./entities/rust.md). They are first-class\ncapabilities, not a ranking and not an exclusive list. The profiles record the\nworkloads each language should lead, the costs that need justification,\ncandidate toolchain implementations, and conditions that point elsewhere.\n\n[Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md)\nis the accepted selection pattern. Its starting map is:\n\n| Workload | First language to evaluate | Main reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Browser UI and web interaction | TypeScript | Native platform ecosystem plus static contracts |\n| AI, data, experimentation, and orchestration | Python | Dominant domain ecosystem and fast iteration |\n| Network services, operational workers, distributable CLIs, and daemons | Go | Simple deployment, concurrency, strong standard tooling |\n| Parsers, indexes, storage/native cores, embedded/constrained components, and strict resource or latency boundaries | Rust | Memory safety without a garbage collector and precise resource control |\n\nThese are defaults for where evaluation starts, not automatic final answers.\nMCP is a protocol rather than a language category. Platform-owned work can\nroute outside the four-language core—for example Swift, Kotlin, CUDA/C++, or\nC#—when that ecosystem is itself a requirement.\n\nEvery selected stack must supply the same durable quality contract: declared\nand pinned runtime/toolchain policy, manifest and lockfile where supported,\ndeterministic format/check/test/build commands, strict static checks,\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n### Diagnostics\n\n- code: BRAIN_TEMPORAL_HISTORY_INVALID\n- message: Authored temporal history is invalid; its prose cannot supply a settled answer: projects/anvil-redux.md, self-model/working-with-vik.md\n- recovery: Repair the affected authored history through the wiki Steward; do not use its prose as a settled temporal answer.\n- code: BRAIN_TEMPORAL_UNCERTAIN\n- message: Relevant context has no verified claim for the requested time or has unspecified temporal state: entities/anvil-primitives.md, entities/go.md, entities/javascript-and-typescript.md (+30 more)\n- recovery: Use explicit temporal or source follow-up before claiming current or dated applicability; do not invent missing times.\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `entities/python.md`, `entities/manifest.md`, `patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md`, `projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md`\n- temporal_query: {\"view\":\"current\",\"request_time\":\"2026-09-06T12:41:59Z\",\"world_at\":\"2026-09-06T12:41:59Z\",\"known_at\":\"2026-09-06T12:41:59Z\"}\n",
