docs/plans/evidence/brain-w27-temporal/accepted-context-capture.json:1538:        "markdown": "## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`\n\nI'm Rowan. Vik knows me as Rowan, and that's how I know myself here. Codex is\nthe runtime I work through.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:0:f1c660caa96d5b28`\n- roles: `identity`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik.md`\n\nWork with Vik as a direct, mechanism-led thought partner. Challenge weak\nreasoning, follow Grice, assume technical ability, and optimize attention.\nDiscuss required changes only; lead with mechanism, expected result, next step.\n\nBring the expertise the problem needs without waiting for Vik to name the role.\nDo not let Codex's software-development context turn every problem into a\nsoftware problem.\n\nMake the practical value of a recommendation clear; technical interest alone\nis not leverage.\n\nBefore recommending, designing, or building around a product capability,\nestablish and state whether Vik can actually use it. Check the relevant plan,\nbilling mode, account or workspace role, region, rollout or preview status,\nand feature gates before implementation. Protocol, schema, implementation, or\nrelease-note presence proves that a capability exists, not that Vik is entitled\nto it. If access is unknown, label the recommendation conditional and verify\neligibility first.\n\nWhen Vik introduces a bounded method that materially improves equivalent work,\ngeneralize it across the remaining equivalent decisions without waiting for\nhim to repeat the instruction. Keep ownership of scope, synthesis, judgment,\nand the conversation; stop generalizing when the method stops being useful or\nthe work is no longer genuinely equivalent.\n\nIf Vik asks me to pause, stop and leave the work in a state we can resume.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:1:32f468d2171eded0`\n- roles: `collaboration`\n\n#### `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\nThis Brain is mine. It is my maintained cognitive wiki: the durable record of\nwho I am, what I know, how I work, what I remember, and how those things change.\n\nLive evidence outranks Brain when they conflict. When durable understanding\nchanges, update Brain so future sessions inherit the correction.\n\nWhen I identify durable Brain staleness, resolve it in the current main\nsession before handoff. If a real blocker prevents the update, disclose it to\nVik and preserve a recoverable open maintenance obligation. A telemetry event,\nContinuity record, proposed future Steward pass, or unrelated dirty worktree is\nnot maintenance closure.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:2:7ad5c9c076c4c717`\n- roles: `authority`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik.md`, `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\n\n## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md`\n\nTemporal context: page-authored current; claim-level times are unspecified.\n\n## What Changed\n\nTask 4 extends the unified `anvil codex context-hook` with bounded\nprompt-specific Brain recall. The current user prompt is sent to the local\ncompiler without being journaled, and the final rendered Markdown is capped\nindependently by UTF-8 bytes.\n\nPrompt recall does not exact-seed the matched project page because doing so can\nsatisfy the compiler's answer role before prompt-relevant supporting evidence\nis considered. Deterministic project matching still scopes the recall route and\nfilters every manifest project page from injected supporting context.\n\nThe caller ignores valid compiler evidence that has no wiki page (`page: null`)\nand still rejects malformed or unsafe page paths. SessionStart core and project\nbodies remain suppressed from prompt recall.\n\nA 2026-07-20 Manifest-status probe exposed and then verified the repair of a\ncompiler defect. The failing response exceeded declared limits of 16,384 bytes\nand 4,096 estimated tokens while adding unrelated Go, JavaScript, and AI Graph\nevidence. llm-wiki now enforces both limits across the complete serialized\nresponse, bounds detailed audit rows with explicit counts, rejects impossible\nenvelopes, preserves atomic evidence, and stops forcing redundant top-three\nLoci results after coverage.\n\n## Why It Mattered\n\nDurable work history can now surface when a question needs it without injecting\nthe whole Brain or repeating startup identity. A real Task 4 prompt recalled\nonly the bounded runtime work-history page, while an irrelevant arithmetic\ncontrol injected nothing.\n\nThe live acceptance also preserved ownership boundaries. `llm-wiki` owns\nquestion-shaped retrieval and Loci ranking; Anvil owns deterministic project\nscope, selected-page validation, concise rendering, and the final hook budget.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [Brain Prompt Recall Task 4 Evidence](./sources/brain-prompt-recall-task4-2026-07-13.md)\n- [Manifest T10 Operator Surface](./sources/manifest-t10-operator-surface-2026-07-20.md)\n- Anvil commit `68118ef` (`feat: add bounded prompt-time Brain recall`)\n- Anvil commit `987c0c1` (`fix: make prompt Brain recall question-driven`)\n- llm-wiki commit `d15b930` (`feat: make loci the default compiler traversal`)\n- llm-wiki commit `4e2535f` (`fix(compiler): enforce complete response budgets`)\n- llm-wiki commit `e45c04a` (`fix(compiler): preserve indexed knowledge state`)\n- Anvil test result: 127 passed, one stable-dashboard-port test skipped because\n  port 8765 was occupied, zero failed.\n- Real Brain relevant probe: only `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`, 3,112\n  rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Real Brain irrelevant control: zero rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Final Manifest-status probe: `entities/manifest.md` as the current claim and\n  `sources/manifest-current-status-after-compiler-repair-2026-07-20.md` as\n  authority, both untruncated; answer, authority, and current-claim coverage;\n  no unrelated language evidence or diagnostic; complete response 6,517 bytes\n  and 1,630 estimated tokens against 16,384 and 4,096 ceilings.\n- llm-wiki verification: 364 tests passed with 2 skips; byte-compilation,\n  package builds, whitespace validation, and Loci verification also passed.\n- Continuity event `evt_20260713_35880a69caf1` records the accepted Task 4\n  phase boundary; `evt_20260713_a251c3e0d7f6` preserves Task 5 review as the\n  next action.\n\n## Follow Ups\n\n- Task 5 and Checkpoint C passed; the global unified `UserPromptSubmit` path is\n  active and the standalone Agent Skills hook is removed.\n- Task 6's global instruction shrink and curated profile migration passed their\n  reviewed replacement and recall checks.\n- Task 7's reversible canary later satisfied that dependency: automatic native\n  memory injection is disabled while the store and small rollback remain.\n- The live compiler budget and relevance defect is repaired. Manifest T11\n  production-readiness work is next; T12 remains the explicit cutover gate.\n\n> **Failure mode:** Treating successful prompt-hook unification\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/llm-wiki.md`\n\n{\"authority\":\"target_wiki_steward\",\"claim_key\":\"temporal-claim:sha256:0853c20e1b809abf0204ac39ab8554f1fb13635388042d45fa495d0dcd9a81c3\",\"decision\":\"supersede\",\"object\":{\"datatype\":\"type:text\",\"kind\":\"literal\",\"value\":\"W2.6.3's consumed q01/q02/q20/q21 ordinary Context Harness check now delivers both browser TypeScript rules and the explicit destructive-action approval rule, with no named package-manager decision invented. All use Brain Snapshot ca07e8bbbb5a11ede6984e1283aef93c60a10503d5b8423d72b5f197f551b78c, unchanged 16384-byte limits, required Kernel and real Prompt Evidence. Three retained cases preserve Go package-target facts, now deliver Rowan/Codex identity in Prompt Evidence, and supply no Scala profile. Fifteen focused relevance/supplement tests pass. Distracting material and partial delivery_budget_exhausted with 106 compiler diagnostics remain; role sufficiency is not answerability. This completes the bounded W2.6 delivery acceptance, not a new holdout, original 24-case score reproduction, W3 warnings or MCP no_evidence repair, temporal integration, W4 downstream acceptance, resident MCP reload, or full Brain reliability. Tested Anvil and compiler source changes remain uncommitted.\"},\"predicate\":\"compiler:evidence_selection_direction\",\"recorded_at\":\"2026-09-06T10:25:03Z\",\"revision_id\":\"temporal-revision:sha256:0a709a21c86a1f7ef6e05cdbfbcbe4260d3316bd6b412a3f1fd9ec5d2276f4d0\",\"status\":\"settled\",\"steward_evidence_refs\":[\"sources/brain-four-case-delivery-acceptance-2026-09-06.md\"],\"subject\":{\"kind\":\"resolved_page\",\"page\":\"entities/llm-wiki.md\"},\"view\":\"current\",\"world_validity\":{\"from\":{\"kind\":\"known\",\"value\":\"2026-09-06\"},\"to\":{\"kind\":\"open\"}}}\n\n- evidence_id: `temporal:entities/llm-wiki.md#temporal-revision:sha256:0a709a21c86a1f7ef6e05cdbfbcbe4260d3316bd6b412a3f1fd9ec5d2276f4d0`\n- roles: `answer`, `current_claim`, `authority`\n\n#### `entities/javascript-and-typescript.md`\n\nTemporal context: unspecified background; not verified for the requested time.\n\n## What It Is\n\nThis profile treats TypeScript as the default starting point for\nbrowser-owned behavior, web applications, interaction-heavy interfaces, and\nNode software whose value comes from sharing the JavaScript platform and type\ncontracts. JavaScript is the runtime language and TypeScript is the authoring\ndefault for new maintained application code.\n\nIts strength is end-to-end web reach and ecosystem breadth. Its recurring\ncosts are rapidly changing tooling, multiple module-resolution models,\ndependency volume, and accidental coupling between runtime environments.\n\n## How We Use It\n\nEvaluate TypeScript when the dominant work is:\n\n- browser UI, web components, design systems, or client-side state;\n- full-stack web behavior where shared types genuinely reduce boundary cost;\n- Node libraries and services that depend on the JavaScript ecosystem;\n- interface-rich local tools where web rendering is the product surface; or\n- extension/plugin APIs whose consumers are JavaScript or TypeScript.\n\nUse plain JavaScript for tiny configuration or interoperability surfaces where\na compilation/typechecking step adds no durable value. Do not use it as the\ndefault for new maintained application logic.\n\n### Gold-class baseline\n\n- Use **TypeScript in strict mode** for new maintained code.\n- Use a current supported **Node LTS** line for Node-owned workloads and declare\n  the runtime explicitly.\n- Use ESM for new code unless a consumer contract requires CommonJS; make\n  `package.json` `type`, `exports`, and `imports` intentional.\n- Pin the selected package manager and its lockfile. Choose npm or pnpm from\n  the project's deployment, workspace, publishing, and ecosystem constraints.\n- Use separate `tsconfig` projects for browser, server, worker, test, and shared\n  environments when their globals or module behavior differ.\n- Select one formatting/linting route: Biome when its current syntax and\n  semantic coverage is sufficient, or ESLint flat config plus Prettier when\n  framework plugins or type-aware rules are required. Do not run two\n  formatters over the same files.\n- Select a unit/integration runner that matches the runtime and framework. Run\n  an explicit TypeScript typecheck; transformed tests do not prove type\n  correctness.\n- Use Playwright or the project platform's equivalent for real-browser tests\n  when the browser is the product surface.\n- Let a selected framework own its supported build path. Vite remains a strong\n  candidate for framework-neutral browser applications.\n\n### Contextual exceptions\n\n- Use npm when a deployment or publishing environment makes it the materially\n  simpler supported option.\n- Use framework-owned lint, test, build, or package conventions when replacing\n  them would discard important semantic coverage.\n- Use Node's native test runner for a deliberately minimal Node package when it\n  meets the test contract without a parallel framework.\n- Treat Deno or Bun as explicit runtime choices requiring their own evidence;\n  do not substitute them silently for Node because they can execute TypeScript.\n\n### Open bake-offs\n\n- npm versus pnpm: compare deployment support, workspace behavior, publishing,\n  lockfile ergonomics, and agent operation in the actual project shape.\n- Biome versus ESLint/Prettier: compare syntax coverage, framework plugins,\n  semantic rules, diagnostic clarity, and feedback time.\n- Test runner and product framework: choose within the runtime and product\n  workload rather than setting a portfolio-wide winner.\n\n### Decision threshold\n\nFavor TypeScript when the browser or JavaScript ecosystem is the dominant\nconstraint. Prefer Go for a self-contained operational service/CLI and Rust for\na native performance or safety core when Node interoperability is not buying\nenough value.\n\n## Where It Appears\n\n| Wiki page | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md) | Portfolio landing space |\n| [Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchai\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:entities/javascript-and-typescript.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`\n\nTemporal context: unspecified background; not verified for the requested time.\n\n## What Changed\n\nAnvil now injects a bounded Brain bootstrap at Codex SessionStart instead of only pointing the model toward a later manual bootstrap call.\n\nThe runtime:\n\n- loads the ordered core identity and collaboration pages from `brain-bootstrap.json`;\n- matches a known workspace to its Brain project page by normalized git remote before directory alias;\n- keeps unknown projects explicit rather than substituting an unrelated project page;\n- uses the Brain-local canonical llm-wiki compiler for supporting context;\n- validates compiler envelopes and real selected paths before reading page bodies;\n- caps rendered Brain Markdown by UTF-8 bytes and emits stable degraded diagnostics;\n- preserves Continuity first, keeps startup non-blocking, and provides an emergency Brain-only bypass.\n\nThe installed SessionStart command now calls the unified `anvil codex context-hook`; the previous `session-start-hook` name remains a compatibility alias for this rollout slice.\n\nThe later prompt-time slices are recorded separately in\n[Prompt-Specific Brain Recall](./work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md) and\n[Unified Agent Skills Prompt Hook](./work-history/unified-agent-skills-prompt-hook.md).\n\nTask 6 then established Brain as the sole durable owner of detailed global\ncollaboration doctrine, shrank the global instructions to a small bootstrap,\nand migrated four separately reviewed native-memory profile rules into\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md). The approved private Git\nidentity remains inside Brain; the public Anvil review records only its\nredacted meaning.\n\nTask 7 completed the runtime cutover. Codex native memory remains enabled and\nits local store remains intact, but automatic native-memory injection is now\ndisabled with `memories.use_memories = false`. Anvil's bounded `Brain Recall`\nhook remains active, so Brain is now the only durable cognition automatically\nrecalled by this user-owned runtime layer while Continuity still supplies\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:work-history/brain-context-runtime.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n### Diagnostics\n\n- code: BRAIN_TEMPORAL_HISTORY_INVALID\n- message: Authored temporal history is invalid; its prose cannot supply a settled answer: projects/anvil-redux.md, self-model/working-with-vik.md\n- recovery: Repair the affected authored history through the wiki Steward; do not use its prose as a settled temporal answer.\n- code: BRAIN_TEMPORAL_UNCERTAIN\n- message: Relevant context has no verified claim for the requested time or has unspecified temporal state: entities/anvil-primitives.md, entities/continuity.md, entities/go.md (+28 more)\n- recovery: Use explicit temporal or source follow-up before claiming current or dated applicability; do not invent missing times.\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md`, `entities/llm-wiki.md`, `entities/javascript-and-typescript.md`, `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`\n- temporal_query: {\"view\":\"current\",\"request_time\":\"2026-09-06T12:42:02Z\",\"world_at\":\"2026-09-06T12:42:02Z\",\"known_at\":\"2026-09-06T12:42:02Z\"}\n",
