lus browser, property, race, fuzz, or\n  concurrency tests where the risk model calls for them;\n- dependency vulnerability and supply-chain review;\n- a reproducible\n\n[truncated]"
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-2.json:644:              "content": "## What Changed\n\nTask 4 extends the unified `anvil codex context-hook` with bounded\nprompt-specific Brain recall. The current user prompt is sent to the local\ncompiler without being journaled, and the final rendered Markdown is capped\nindependently by UTF-8 bytes.\n\nPrompt recall does not exact-seed the matched project page because doing so can\nsatisfy the compiler's answer role before prompt-relevant supporting evidence\nis considered. Deterministic project matching still scopes the recall route and\nfilters every manifest project page from injected supporting context.\n\nThe caller ignores valid compiler evidence that has no wiki page (`page: null`)\nand still rejects malformed or unsafe page paths. SessionStart core and project\nbodies remain suppressed from prompt recall.\n\nA 2026-07-20 Manifest-status probe exposed and then verified the repair of a\ncompiler defect. The failing response exceeded declared limits of 16,384 bytes\nand 4,096 estimated tokens while adding unrelated Go, JavaScript, and AI Graph\nevidence. llm-wiki now enforces both limits across the complete serialized\nresponse, bounds detailed audit rows with explicit counts, rejects impossible\nenvelopes, preserves atomic evidence, and stops forcing redundant top-three\nLoci results after coverage.\n\n## Why It Mattered\n\nDurable work history can now surface when a question needs it without injecting\nthe whole Brain or repeating startup identity. A real Task 4 prompt recalled\nonly the bounded runtime work-history page, while an irrelevant arithmetic\ncontrol injected nothing.\n\nThe live acceptance also preserved ownership boundaries. `llm-wiki` owns\nquestion-shaped retrieval and Loci ranking; Anvil owns deterministic project\nscope, selected-page validation, concise rendering, and the final hook budget.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [Brain Prompt Recall Task 4 Evidence](./sources/brain-prompt-recall-task4-2026-07-13.md)\n- [Manifest T10 Operator Surface](./sources/manifest-t10-operator-surface-2026-07-20.md)\n- Anvil commit `68118ef` (`feat: add bounded prompt-time Brain recall`)\n- Anvil commit `987c0c1` (`fix: make prompt Brain recall question-driven`)\n- llm-wiki commit `d15b930` (`feat: make loci the default compiler traversal`)\n- llm-wiki commit `4e2535f` (`fix(compiler): enforce complete response budgets`)\n- llm-wiki commit `e45c04a` (`fix(compiler): preserve indexed knowledge state`)\n- Anvil test result: 127 passed, one stable-dashboard-port test skipped because\n  port 8765 was occupied, zero failed.\n- Real Brain relevant probe: only `work-history/brain-context-runtime.md`, 3,112\n  rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Real Brain irrelevant control: zero rendered bytes, no diagnostic.\n- Final Manifest-status probe: `entities/manifest.md` as the current claim and\n  `sources/manifest-current-status-after-compiler-repair-2026-07-20.md` as\n  authority, both untruncated; answer, authority, and current-claim coverage;\n  no unrelated language evidence or diagnostic; complete response 6,517 bytes\n  and 1,630 estimated tokens against 16,384 and 4,096 ceilings.\n- llm-wiki verification: 364 tests passed with 2 skips; byte-compilation,\n  package builds, whitespace validation, and Loci verification also passed.\n- Continuity event `evt_20260713_35880a69caf1` records the accepted Task 4\n  phase boundary; `evt_20260713_a251c3e0d7f6` preserves Task 5 review as the\n  next action.\n\n## Follow Ups\n\n- Task 5 and Checkpoint C passed; the global unified `UserPromptSubmit` path is\n  active and the standalone Agent Skills hook is removed.\n- Task 6's global instruction shrink and curated profile migration passed their\n  reviewed replacement and recall checks.\n- Task 7's reversible canary later satisfied that dependency: automatic native\n  memory injection is disabled while the store and small rollback remain.\n- The live compiler budget and relevance defect is repaired. Manifest T11\n  production-readiness work is next; T12 remains the explicit cutover gate.\n\n> **Failure mode:** Treating successful prompt-hook unification\n\n[truncated]"
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-2.json:654:              "content": "> **Source type:** implementation handoff, repository evidence, Manifest state, and live acceptance probe\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-20\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Brain compiler budget and relevance repair\n\nThe Brain recall branch spawned from Manifest T10 is complete.\n\nllm-wiki commit `4e2535f` makes `max_bytes` and optional\n`max_estimated_tokens` apply to the complete serialized compiler response. It\nbounds detailed omission and diagnostic rows while preserving total and\nreturned counts, rejects irreducible envelopes with `BUDGET_TOO_SMALL`, keeps\natomic evidence indivisible, and removes the rule that forced the first three\nLoci results into selected evidence after required roles were already covered.\n\nThe full llm-wiki suite passed 363 tests with 2 skips. Python byte-compilation,\nsource-distribution and wheel builds, whitespace validation, and Loci\nverification of 1,204 indexed symbols also passed.\n\nThe live Codex Brain query \"What is the current Manifest implementation status\nand what should happen next?\" returned only\n`sources/manifest-t10-operator-surface-2026-07-20.md`. The evidence was not\ntruncated, no unrelated language-project evidence was selected, and no\ndiagnostic was emitted. The complete response measured 5,309 bytes and 1,328\nestimated tokens against ceilings of 16,384 bytes and 4,096 estimated tokens.\nIt preserved visibility of 207 total omissions through 16 bounded detail rows.\n\nManifest event `event_15ab3d9c-e401-4983-b6ec-3b55b59fa94f` marks work\n`work_bd4681c9-f4b0-4978-9e31-782c3cf5a7de` completed with commit and live-test\nevidence. Continuity event `evt_20260720_953121b19de6` now points the compact\nnext action back to Manifest T11 production readiness. T12 remains the only\nauthority-cutover gate."
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-2.json:671:              "content": "## Current Orientation\n\nAnvil Redux is Vik's personal, Codex-first cognitive harness. Its operational\ncapabilities are host-neutral at the core, MCP-first for agents, and CLI-native\nfor human/operator paths. It exists to turn creative intent into excellent\nworking systems through the Spark -> Shape -> Steel arc while preserving\nthought partnership, continuity, implementation discipline, verification, and\nadaptation.\n\n[Brain](./entities/brain.md) is the durable cognitive center of this system.\nAnvil is the broader personal ecosystem and harness that makes Brain\noperational. Its current implementation coordinates supporting surfaces such\nas:\n\n- [Continuity](./entities/continuity.md) for one disposable hot resume\n  handoff;\n- [Manifest](./entities/manifest.md), active in its V2 Git-backed graph form\n  while V1 remains retired history;\n- [Loci](./entities/loci.md) for codebase navigation and symbol/section\n  retrieval;\n- [Anvil MCP](./entities/anvil-mcp.md), skills, hooks, configuration, and docs\n  for agent-facing operation and maintenance.\n\nThe current Brain shape is intentionally plain llm-wiki. Anvil owns setup metadata, routing, bootstrap, and tool orchestration. llm-wiki owns the scaffold, page contract, graph/context tooling, lint, render, and wiki operating rules.\n\nBrain is the maintained cognitive wiki for the agent. It should hold the current synthesized understanding of Anvil work. Live repo evidence, tools, and conversation are inputs that keep Brain synchronized; conflicts should trigger Brain maintenance.\n\nOn 2026-08-11, Anvil temporal maintenance v2 entered its first bounded\nregistered-Brain dogfood after its shadow safety traces passed. This is one\nSteward-accepted historical milestone; broader automatic use remains pending\nVik's review.\n\nWithin Anvil, unqualified knowledge language means Brain unless another store\nis explicitly named. Operational evidence can remain in journals, source\narchives, or proposed mining stores without becoming a competing knowledge\nsystem.\n\nFor Anvil product-direction work, begin from current canon and the live\nambition. Existing Brain, Continuity, MCP, skill, hook, eval, and workflow\nmachinery are carriers, not automatic roadmap drivers. Prefer concrete lived-use\nslices that make Anvil more useful over self-referential governance machinery\nwhen both could answer the same pressure.\n\nManifest V2 is active product work. It uses one repository-owned Objective\ngraph across Planning and Implementation, one shared graph module, a stateless\nlocal MCP adapter for Rowan, and the retained React Flow Human frontend for\nVik. Git is its durable carrier and history. V1's removed SQLite, Ledger,\nlifecycle, and dashboard implementation remains historical lineage; dormant V1\nruntime files do not govern V2.\n\nThe approved normal runtime is deliberately small. Session start, resume, and\ncompact recovery bind the workspace and load the disposable handoff plus Brain\ncore context. Each prompt gets prompt-specific Brain recall and cheap automatic\nevidence. Trusted routing supplies workspace and principal identity, and Loci\nis on demand.\nFrom the 2026-08-13 subtractive cutover until 2026-09-04, Stop launched nothing\nfor Brain maintenance. Candidate queues, deferred handoffs, the separate\nSteward child, and automatic judge paths remain removed. Commit `0da911e`\nrestored only a deterministic closure guard: Stop reads the bound session's\nBrain usage journal and blocks handoff while a `stale`, `wrong`, or\n`missed_update` incident lacks an explicitly related `updated` or `not_needed`\nresolution. The current main Codex session remains the sole Brain Steward.\n\nThe guard closes the task-end enforcement gap without restoring an automatic\nexecutor. The explicit `anvil review weekly` command is a separate read-only\noperator review. Its absence is a missing review surface, not a detection,\nmaintenance-closure, or repair gap.\n\n[Blueprint](./entities/blueprint.md), repository-command policing, unbounded or\nrecursive Steward\n\n[truncated]"
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-2.json:761:              "content": "## What Changed\n\nAnvil now injects a bounded Brain bootstrap at Codex SessionStart instead of only pointing the model toward a later manual bootstrap call.\n\nThe runtime:\n\n- loads the ordered core identity and collaboration pages from `brain-bootstrap.json`;\n- matches a known workspace to its Brain project page by normalized git remote before directory alias;\n- keeps unknown projects explicit rather than substituting an unrelated project page;\n- uses the Brain-local canonical llm-wiki compiler for supporting context;\n- validates compiler envelopes and real selected paths before reading page bodies;\n- caps rendered Brain Markdown by UTF-8 bytes and emits stable degraded diagnostics;\n- preserves Continuity first, keeps startup non-blocking, and provides an emergency Brain-only bypass.\n\nThe installed SessionStart command now calls the unified `anvil codex context-hook`; the previous `session-start-hook` name remains a compatibility alias for this rollout slice.\n\nThe later prompt-time slices are recorded separately in\n[Prompt-Specific Brain Recall](./work-history/brain-prompt-recall.md) and\n[Unified Agent Skills Prompt Hook](./work-history/unified-agent-skills-prompt-hook.md).\n\nTask 6 then established Brain as the sole durable owner of detailed global\ncollaboration doctrine, shrank the global instructions to a small bootstrap,\nand migrated four separately reviewed native-memory profile rules into\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md). The approved private Git\nidentity remains inside Brain; the public Anvil review records only its\nredacted meaning.\n\nTask 7 completed the runtime cutover. Codex native memory remains enabled and\nits local store remains intact, but automatic native-memory injection is now\ndisabled with `memories.use_memories = false`. Anvil's bounded `Brain Recall`\nhook remains active, so Brain is now the only durable cognition automatically\nrecalled by this user-owned runtime layer while Continuity still supplies hot\nworking state.\n\nThe 2026-07-16 standing-guidance follow-up closes the ordinary-prompt clarity\ngap without manipulating retrieval queries. The optional version 1 manifest\nextension declares a compact subset of core pages plus a dedicated hard byte\nlimit. On every `UserPromptSubmit`, Anvil validates and renders that guidance\nfirst, runs the existing exact-prompt llm-wiki recall once, removes duplicate\nguidance selections, and preserves the existing total prompt cap. Old\nmanifests remain valid with standing guidance disabled. The canonical\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md) page is now compact;\ntask-specific rules remain durable on its linked situational page rather than\nbeing injected into every turn.\n\nThe 2026-07-18 repair exposed a maintenance gap. Brain already described Anvil\nas producing excellent working systems, but the compact standing page omitted\nthe explicit production-grade foundation rule. Adding it without compiling the\nreal standing section then grew that section from 2,939 to 3,343 bytes, beyond\nthe reviewed 3,072-byte limit, so prompt recall degraded. The standing page was\ncompacted without weakening the rule, and Brain lint now mirrors Anvil's\nfrontmatter stripping, section rendering, and UTF-8 byte measurement."
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-2.json:942:              "content": "> **Source type:** Session synthesis and explicit user direction\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Gold-Class Language Tooling Direction\n\nVik set a portfolio-wide direction for future software work:\n\n- choose the right language and tooling for the job across every future\n  project, rather than standardising accidentally around whichever repository\n  already exists;\n- treat current repositories as historical examples and migration inputs, not\n  as authoritative evidence of what the preferred stack should be;\n- maintain a dedicated space in Rowan's Brain for language fit, gold-class\n  toolchains, exceptions, and current evidence so stack selection becomes a\n  normal part of new-project reasoning;\n- begin with Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, and Rust;\n- treat Rust as a definite near-term Anvil language, not speculative future\n  preparation; and\n- complete trustworthy JavaScript/TypeScript and Cargo-aware Rust dependency\n  resolution in Loci before building resolved symbol references and definite\n  cross-file calls on top of the import graph.\n\nThe intended outcome is not one universal language. It is a small, deliberate\nportfolio in which each language has a clear default role, a maintained\ntoolchain, known costs, and an explicit threshold for choosing it.\n\nRelated Continuity decision: `evt_20260717_dc0183dd5972`."
