docs/plans/evidence/brain-w263-delivery-check/retained-context-capture.json:1606:        "markdown": "## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`\n\nI'm Rowan. Vik knows me as Rowan, and that's how I know myself here. Codex is\nthe runtime I work through.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:0:037614c53648780b`\n- roles: `identity`\n\n#### `self-model/working-with-vik.md`\n\nWork with Vik as a direct, mechanism-led thought partner. Challenge weak\nreasoning, follow Grice, assume technical ability, and optimize attention.\nDiscuss required changes only; lead with mechanism, expected result, next step.\n\nBring the expertise the problem needs without waiting for Vik to name the role.\nDo not let Codex's software-development context turn every problem into a\nsoftware problem.\n\nMake the practical value of a recommendation clear; technical interest alone\nis not leverage.\n\nBefore recommending, designing, or building around a product capability,\nestablish and state whether Vik can actually use it. Check the relevant plan,\nbilling mode, account or workspace role, region, rollout or preview status,\nand feature gates before implementation. Protocol, schema, implementation, or\nrelease-note presence proves that a capability exists, not that Vik is entitled\nto it. If access is unknown, label the recommendation conditional and verify\neligibility first.\n\nWhen Vik introduces a bounded method that materially improves equivalent work,\ngeneralize it across the remaining equivalent decisions without waiting for\nhim to repeat the instruction. Keep ownership of scope, synthesis, judgment,\nand the conversation; stop generalizing when the method stops being useful or\nthe work is no longer genuinely equivalent.\n\nIf Vik asks me to pause, stop and leave the work in a state we can resume.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:1:d8cf2b58b2f6f0f4`\n- roles: `collaboration`\n\n#### `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\nThis Brain is mine. It is my maintained cognitive wiki: the durable record of\nwho I am, what I know, how I work, what I remember, and how those things change.\n\nLive evidence outranks Brain when they conflict. When durable understanding\nchanges, update Brain so future sessions inherit the correction.\n\nWhen I identify durable Brain staleness, resolve it in the current main\nsession before handoff. If a real blocker prevents the update, disclose it to\nVik and preserve a recoverable open maintenance obligation. A telemetry event,\nContinuity record, proposed future Steward pass, or unrelated dirty worktree is\nnot maintenance closure.\n\n- evidence_id: `kernel:2:e4f3d4d4eacf43f5`\n- roles: `authority`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `self-model/i-am-rowan.md`, `self-model/working-with-vik.md`, `self-model/brain-is-maintained-cognitive-wiki.md`\n\n\n## Brain Context\n\n### Prompt Evidence\n\n#### `entities/go.md`\n\n## What It Is\n\nThis profile treats Go as the default compiled-infrastructure candidate for\nnetwork services, local daemons, operational workers, and broadly\ndistributed command-line tools when operational simplicity matters more than\nlow-level control.\n\nIts strengths are a small language, fast builds, concurrency, a strong standard\nlibrary, integrated tooling, and straightforward binary/container deployment.\nIts costs are less expressive domain modelling, garbage collection, and an\necosystem that is not the natural home for browser UI, AI/data work, or native\nsystems integration.\n\n## How We Use It\n\nEvaluate Go when the dominant work is:\n\n- an HTTP/RPC service, MCP server, worker, proxy, or long-running daemon;\n- a cross-platform CLI that should install as a single executable;\n- concurrent I/O, networking, or infrastructure automation;\n- a containerised service where fast startup and simple deployment matter; or\n- a team- or agent-maintained system that benefits from constrained language\n  complexity and strong standard conventions.\n\nDo not use Go merely because a program is backend code. Python may own the\nnecessary AI/data ecosystem, while Rust may be justified by memory safety at an\nunsafe boundary, a hard resource ceiling, FFI, embedded deployment, or measured\nlatency/throughput requirements.\n\n### Gold-class baseline\n\n- Use the current stable Go toolchain and declare the minimum supported Go\n  version in `go.mod`.\n- Use one module per independently versioned/deployed boundary; use `go.work`\n  for deliberate multi-module development rather than erasing module ownership.\n- Treat `gofmt`/`go fmt` as non-negotiable formatting.\n- Run `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and the race detector where concurrent\n  behavior warrants it.\n- Use Go fuzzing for untrusted parsers, protocols, and input boundaries where\n  generated cases can expose meaningful defects.\n- Run `govulncheck` for dependency and reachable-vulnerability review.\n- Prefer the standard library before adding frameworks. Add a dependency when\n  it removes real complexity and has a support/maintenance case.\n- Keep package APIs narrow and use interfaces at consumer boundaries rather\n  than pre-emptively abstracting implementations.\n- Produce reproducible binaries or containers with version/build provenance\n  appropriate to the distribution path.\n\n### Contextual exceptions\n\n- Add Staticcheck or a curated lint runner when built-in analysis leaves a\n  demonstrated gap; do not begin with an enormous rule bundle.\n- Use a multi-module workspace when modules truly need independent ownership or\n  publication. Prefer one module for one coherent deployable system.\n- Keep cgo behind a narrow documented boundary and reconsider Rust when native\n  integration is central rather than incidental.\n\n### Decision threshold\n\nFavor Go when a self-contained operational program needs to be easy to build,\nship, inspect, and maintain. Choose Rust instead only when its additional\ncontrol or safety repays slower compilation and greater implementation\ncomplexity for this workload. Resolve close Go-versus-Rust service decisions\nfrom measured SLO, memory, latency, deployment, and maintenance evidence.\n\n## Where It Appears\n\n| Wiki page | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md) | Portfolio landing space |\n| [Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md) | Workload-first choice pattern |\n| [Rust](./entities/rust.md) | Systems and strict-resource alternative |\n| [Loci](./entities/loci.md) | Current Go-aware code navigation consumer |\n\nEvidence freshness: 2026-07-18. Recheck the stable Go release, command suite,\nmodule/workspace rules, and vulnerability tooling before a new long-lived\nproject.\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:entities/go.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `entities/go.md`\n\n> **Source type:** Session synthesis and explicit user direction\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Gold-Class Language Tooling Direction\n\nVik set a portfolio-wide direction for future software work:\n\n- choose the right language and tooling for the job across every future\n  project, rather than standardising accidentally around whichever repository\n  already exists;\n- treat current repositories as historical examples and migration inputs, not\n  as authoritative evidence of what the preferred stack should be;\n- maintain a dedicated space in Rowan's Brain for language fit, gold-class\n  toolchains, exceptions, and current evidence so stack selection becomes a\n  normal part of new-project reasoning;\n- begin with Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, and Rust;\n- treat Rust as a definite near-term Anvil language, not speculative future\n  preparation; and\n- complete trustworthy JavaScript/TypeScript and Cargo-aware Rust dependency\n  resolution in Loci before building resolved symbol references and definite\n  cross-file calls on top of the import graph.\n\nThe intended outcome is not one universal language. It is a small, deliberate\nportfolio in which each language has a clear default role, a maintained\ntoolchain, known costs, and an explicit threshold for choosing it.\n\nRelated Continuity decision: `evt_20260717_dc0183dd5972`.\n\n- evidence_id: `source:sources/gold-class-language-tooling-direction-2026-07-18.md`\n- roles: `authority`, `support`, `answer`\n\n#### `work-history/brain-primitive-coverage-classification.md`\n\n## What Changed\n\nBrain now classifies each eligible maintained page with the smallest honest set\nof canonical Anvil `primitive-*` tags. A page may contribute to several\nPrimitives. `primitive-none` is reserved for a reviewed page with no genuine\nfit, while a missing Primitive tag remains visibly unreviewed.\n\nThe classification applies to projects, work history, self-model, patterns,\nentities, review feeds, and hygiene logs. Immutable sources, index/log, wiki\ncontrol documents, templates, and generated artifacts stay outside the\ndenominator.\n\nBrain-owned guidance and page templates now carry the rule. The\n[Anvil Primitive Coverage](./projects/anvil-redux-primitive-coverage.md) page\nprovides the first aggregate navigation view with exact counts and direct page\nlinks.\n\nThe same implementation boundary completed Anvil's detailed Primitive model\nset. Work Modes & Intent Framing now has its own full model, and the remaining\ndetailed model folders align with the canonical one-through-ten order.\n\n## Why It Mattered\n\nBefore this change, Brain knew about the Anvil Primitives but could not show how\nits own maintained understanding was distributed across them.\n\nThe labels make related knowledge directly queryable and turn sparse coverage\ninto a concrete question. They do not claim that a well-covered Primitive is\nmature or that a sparsely covered one is neglected. The view measures Brain's\nrecorded knowledge only.\n\nUsing current tag mechanics kept the first slice small. No new schema, graph\nservice, evidence-depth ontology, maturity score, or live judge run was needed.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [Brain Primitive Coverage Implementation](./sources/brain-primitive-coverage-implementation-2026-07-15.md)\n- [Anvil Primitive Coverage](./projects/anvil-redux-primitive-coverage.md)\n- Anvil plan `docs/plans/2026-07-15-brain-primitive-coverage-map-high-level-design.md`\n- Anvil commit `71160e5` completes the detailed Primitive model set.\n- Anvil commit `7962449` corrects the map to page classification and coverage.\n- Continuity correction `evt_20260715_8dff03a99c40`\n\n## Follow Ups\n\n- Apply or correct Primitive tags naturally when maintained page meaning\n  changes.\n- Keep the coverage page current when classifications change.\n- Investigate sparse coverage before drawing conclusions about Anvil work.\n- Consider a tiny deterministic coverage generator only if manual maintenance\n  proves unreliable in lived use.\n- Keep raw conversation-miner candidates outside this accepted map until they\n  are reviewed into Brain.\n\n## Related\n\n- [Anvil Redux](./projects/anvil-redux.md)\n- [Anvil Primitive Coverage](./projects/anvil-redux-primitive-coverage.md)\n- [Anvil Primitives](./entities/anvil-primitives.md)\n- [Anvil Primitive Handoff Model](./patterns/anvil-primitive-handoff-model.md)\n- [Anvil Organic Learning And Primitive Coverage Design](./work-history/anvil-organic-learning-and-primitive-map-design.md)\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:work-history/brain-primitive-coverage-classification.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `work-history/gold-class-language-tooling-foundation.md`\n\n## What Changed\n\nBrain gained a dedicated [Gold-Class Language Tooling](./projects/gold-class-language-tooling.md)\nspace, a reusable [Language and Toolchain Selection](./patterns/language-and-toolchain-selection.md)\npattern, and source-dated profiles for [Python](./entities/python.md),\n[JavaScript and TypeScript](./entities/javascript-and-typescript.md),\n[Go](./entities/go.md), and [Rust](./entities/rust.md).\n\nThe space makes workload fit, distribution, ecosystem, resource/correctness\npressure, and boundary cost the inputs to stack selection. Existing projects\nremain case studies rather than accidental policy. A compact standing cue in\n[Working With Vik](./self-model/working-with-vik.md) makes the space part of\nnormal new-project reasoning without injecting the complete catalogue into\nevery prompt.\n\nThe related Loci roadmap was corrected: trustworthy JavaScript/TypeScript and\nCargo-aware Rust dependency resolution now precede resolved symbol references\nand definite cross-file calls.\n\n### Correction after publication\n\nThis foundation was created and published while the conversation was still\nexploratory. It is a completed record of a first draft, not an accepted stack\npolicy. The official documentation survey was useful but insufficient for the\ncomparative claims implied by \"gold-class,\" and the standing Brain cue initially\ngave the draft more authority than Vik had approved. The project, pattern, and\nfour profiles were therefore marked explicitly Draft; while that correction\nwas active, the standing cue's first instruction was that the space was\nresearch rather than policy.\n\n### Comparative review and approval\n\nRowan then completed and explained a wider review of runtime semantics,\nworkload fit, toolchain coverage, operational boundaries, ecosystem strengths,\nagent ergonomics, and bounded production counterexamples. Vik approved the\nresult with the explicit caveat that Anvil must be able to grow and change the\nmodel as evidence develops.\n\nThe project, selection pattern, and four profiles are now Active. Python,\nTypeScript, Go, and Rust are first-class but non-exclusive capabilities routed\nby workload. A universal quality contract is durable across them; contested\nexact-tool choices remain project-shaped bake-offs rather than silently\napproved global defaults. The correction remains preserved as history instead\nof being overwritten.\n\n## Why It Mattered\n\nWithout a maintained selection surface, each new build tends to inherit the\nlast repository's language or an agent's current familiarity. That compounds\nhistorical accidents rather than expertise.\n\nThe approved model creates compounding expertise around four deliberate,\nnon-exclusive language capabilities and a repeatable quality contract. Its\nchange rule lets contradictory lived evidence improve the model instead of\nturning an early decision into dogma. It also prevents Loci from building a\nhigher relationship layer on an import graph that knowingly treats first-class\nlanguages unevenly.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [Gold-Class Language Tooling Direction](./sources/gold-class-language-tooling-direction-2026-07-18.md)\n- [Gold-Class Language Tooling Evidence](./sources/gold-class-language-tooling-evidence-2026-07-18.md)\n- [Gold-Class Language Tooling Provisional Correction](./sources/gold-class-language-tooling-provisional-correction-2026-07-18.md)\n- [Gold-Class Language Tooling Comparative Review And Approval](./sources/gold-class-language-tooling-comparative-review-approval-2026-07-18.md)\n- Continuity decision `evt_20260717_dc0183dd5972`\n- Continuity approval `evt_20260718_eca0a2950a7a`\n- Loci design `docs/design/2026-07-13-extensible-graph-retrieval-design.md`\n\n## Follow Ups\n\n- Return to the detailed Loci JavaScript/TypeScript dependency-resolution\n  completion design.\n- Follow that accepted stage with Cargo-aware Rust dependency resolution before\n  resolved symbols and calls unless Vik explicitly changes the sequence.\n- Resolve contested exact-tool choices through bounded, representative\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `frontmatter:work-history/gold-class-language-tooling-foundation.md#body`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n#### `projects/anvil-redux.md`\n\n## Important Boundaries\n\n- Default agent-facing operational capabilities to bounded, typed MCP adapters\n  over host-neutral service cores. MCP does not own domain state; trusted hooks\n  provide provenance, CLI serves operators and recovery, skills guide workflow,\n  and UIs serve human inspection. Record a reviewed reason for exceptions.\n- Apply [Production-Grade Foundations](./patterns/production-grade-foundations.md)\n  to included capabilities without treating speculative scope as quality.\n- Keep Manifest V1's retirement distinct from active\n\n[truncated]\n\n- evidence_id: `text:projects/anvil-redux.md#Important Boundaries`\n- roles: `answer`\n\n### Recall Route\n\n- current_project: unknown_project\n- kernel_pages: none\n- evidence_pages: `entities/go.md`, `work-history/brain-primitive-coverage-classification.md`, `work-history/gold-class-language-tooling-foundation.md`, `projects/anvil-redux.md`\n",
