docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/run-01/new.json:2899:            "content": "## How We Use It\n\nUse Loci when codebase navigation matters. Treat its current MCP read surface as\nthe freshness boundary for indexed repository context. A SessionStart hook can\nreport an existing cache or seed an uncached repository, but that startup state\nis not proof that cached content is current.\n\nTreat MCP storage as a client-registration and process boundary, not a tool-call\nchoice. Each host supplies one explicit absolute store root and namespace; the\nserver binds that identity immutably before opening stdio. Codex and Claude use\ndistinct roots and namespaces by default. Missing configuration,\ncross-namespace reuse, unsafe or malformed identity state, and silent adoption\nof a populated legacy root fail closed. Intentional sharing requires both hosts\nto configure the same root and namespace explicitly; repository paths remain\nthe only storage-adjacent input on normal navigation tools.\n\nUse the opt-in traversal surface when repository relationships matter. Trusted\nresolved imports may support file dependency traversal, while unresolved and\nunsupported observations remain inspectable records rather than guessed edges.\nKeep the legacy exact neighbour read contains-only; importing relationships must\nnot silently widen an exact navigation request.\n\nStages 7-11 are complete and accepted. Loci can safely turn bounded contained\n`go.mod` and `go.work` controls into deterministic module and workspace\ncontext, construct stable non-main package nodes, and resolve same-module,\nexplicit workspace, and conservative contained local-replacement imports to\nthose package nodes. Package-target edges, control freshness, incremental\nre-resolution, health counts, generic traversal, and installed MCP output are\nall wired into normal indexing.\n\nThe public resolver distinguishes file and package targets under persisted\ngraph-state schema 3. Go package targets expose `target_kind=\"package\"` and the\neffective import path without pretending the deterministic anchor `.go` file\nis the imported package. Existing Python and JavaScript/TypeScript imports\nremain file targets. Invalid, external, missing, ambiguous, command,\nnested-module, and `internal` visibility outcomes remain explicit and edge-free.\n\nGo resolution remains deliberately bounded: it does not invoke Go or\nrepository code, use the network or ambient workspace, inspect caches,\nimplement minimal version selection, model vendor mode, or evaluate build\nconstraints. The exact neighbour read remains contains-only; import traversal\nuses the generic filtered graph surfaces.\n\nJavaScript/TypeScript resolution now includes bounded project and workspace\ncontrols beyond the earlier relative-import subset. Cargo-aware Rust resolution\nmodels contained packages, targets, modules, path dependencies, visibility,\nand supported configuration without invoking Cargo or guessing external edges.\n\nResolved symbol references can follow definite Python, JavaScript/TypeScript,\nGo, and Rust import bindings to exact indexed definitions. Trustworthy call\nrelationships build only on proven lexical ownership and, for imported calls,\nan accepted resolved-symbol reference. Dynamic or ambiguous dispatch remains\nexplicitly outside the trusted edge set.\n\nWhen exact behavior matters, inspect the live Loci repo and skill for the\ncurrent read tools, host setup, and fallback procedure. Do not copy those\nmutable details into Brain.\n\nBrain and Continuity should not depend on Loci being present; Anvil should\ndegrade cleanly if Loci is missing or stale.\n\n",
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/run-01/new.json:2925:            "content": "> **Source type:** Owner approval, repository review packet, test output, and live MCP verification synthesis\n> **URL:**\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-15\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Loci Stage 6 Import Graph Acceptance\n\n## Accepted Outcome\n\nVik explicitly accepted Loci's extensible graph retrieval Stage 6 on\n2026-07-15. The accepted implementation ends at Loci commit\n`b17adba74b2b8b026a575135c923ccedeb024e0a`; the owner acceptance record is\ncommit `73ecd9e6580d3e8cc2d4796c68751a3b6b476bfc` on `origin/master`.\n\nThe canonical evidence packet is:\n\n```text\n/Users/brummerv/loci/docs/reviews/2026-07-15-extensible-graph-retrieval-stage-6-final-review.md\n```\n\nContinuity event `evt_20260715_ea7f1dfdfc19` records the accepted phase change.\n\n## Durable Capability Boundary\n\nLoci now extracts and persists raw import observations separately from resolved\ngraph edges. It deterministically resolves the approved Python and relative\nJavaScript/TypeScript subset against the current indexed repository, then\nmaterializes only evidence-backed relationships between stable file nodes.\n\nThe accepted MCP boundary exposes bounded resolved and unresolved import\nrecords. Opt-in graph traversal admits trusted `import-resolved` relationships\nalongside exact and declared relationships while continuing to exclude\nheuristic relationships by default. The legacy exact `graph_neighbors` read\nremains contains-only and does not silently widen to imports.\n\nAmbiguous, external, missing-target, Go, and Rust observations remain bounded,\ninspectable unresolved records. They do not become guessed edges or degrade\notherwise healthy graph state. Incremental indexing may reuse unchanged raw\nobservations, but it re-resolves them against the complete current file set so\ntarget additions and deletions are reflected.\n\n## Acceptance Evidence\n\n- 475 Loci tests passed, including 262 focused Stage 6 and graph regressions.\n- 14 targeted `llm-wiki` compatibility tests passed.\n- An isolated installed-wrapper stdio MCP review passed search, outline, exact\n  get, bounded import pagination, outgoing and incoming traversal, exact path\n  evidence, unresolved behavior, and add/delete incremental resolution.\n- Full, fresh-process, and no-change incremental graph-state digests matched.\n- Loci self-indexed with healthy graph state; a five-run comparison observed an\n  approximately 6.7% median full-index increase while producing additional file\n  nodes and graph edges.\n- The frozen `llm-wiki` graph-shape fixture checksum remained unchanged.\n- The final review found no critical or required changes.\n\n## Explicit Non-Authorization\n\nStage 6 acceptance does not approve resolved-reference, call-graph, or later\ngraph-retrieval work. Each requires a separately approved design boundary.",
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/run-01/new.json:4098:            "content": "> **Source type:** Comparative research synthesis and explicit user approval\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Gold-Class Language Tooling Comparative Review And Approval\n\n## Approval\n\nAfter the first-pass language pages were marked provisional, Rowan completed\nand explained a broader comparative review to Vik. Vik approved the direction\nwith one explicit caveat: the model must be able to grow and change as Anvil\nlearns, projects produce evidence, and language ecosystems evolve.\n\nThis approval establishes a revisable operating model, not permanent stack\npolicy. The durable decision is how to reason about language choice and what\nquality every selected stack must provide. It does not freeze every framework,\npackage manager, linter, type checker, or runtime version.\n\nRelated Continuity decision: `evt_20260718_eca0a2950a7a`.\n\n## Evidence Posture\n\nThe review distinguished three kinds of support:\n\n1. **Official facts** establish language, runtime, and tool capabilities.\n2. **First-party production reports and surveys** show bounded experience in\n   real systems; they do not create universal rules.\n3. **Portfolio-routing conclusions** are Rowan and Vik's synthesis from those\n   facts, production cases, Anvil's needs, and explicit trade-offs.\n\nNo single popularity measure, benchmark, or company migration determines the\nright language for another workload.\n\n## Approved Portfolio Model\n\nPython, TypeScript, Go, and Rust are first-class capabilities for future Anvil\nwork. They are neither a ranking nor an exclusive list.\n\nStart from the dominant workload:\n\n| Workload | First language to evaluate | Main reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Browser-owned UI and web interaction | TypeScript | It is native to the JavaScript web platform and adds static authoring contracts. |\n| AI, ML, data, research, evaluation, and model orchestration | Python | Its domain ecosystem and iteration model are the dominant advantage. |\n| Network services, daemons, operational workers, and broadly distributed CLIs | Go | Its standard toolchain, concurrency model, and simple deployment usually minimise operational cost. |\n| Parsers, indexes, storage/native cores, embedded or constrained components, and strict latency/resource boundaries | Rust | Memory safety without a garbage collector and explicit resource control can satisfy requirements the simpler stacks cannot. |\n\nThese are starting points, not automatic answers. Use TypeScript for a Node\nservice when JavaScript ecosystem or shared web contracts dominate. Use Python\nfor a service when the AI/data boundary dominates. Use Rust for a service or\nCLI when measured latency, resource, safety, or native-integration requirements\njustify its additional implementation cost.\n\nUse native platform ecosystems when they own the product surface or required\ncapability: for example Swift on Apple platforms, Kotlin on Android, CUDA/C++\nfor NVIDIA-specific GPU work, or C# in an engine ecosystem that requires it.\nMCP is a protocol boundary, not a language choice; route an MCP implementation\nby the workload underneath it.\n\nMixed-language systems are valid when split at a stable, measurable boundary:\nfor example a Python AI host with a Rust native core, or a Go service shell\naround a Rust engine. Do not introduce a second language merely because it is\nindividually attractive.\n\n## Universal Gold-Class Quality Contract\n\nEvery long-lived project or major subsystem should provide:\n\n- a declared, pinned language/runtime and toolchain policy;\n- a manifest plus a committed lockfile where the ecosystem supports one;\n- deterministic format, static-check, test, and build commands;\n- strict static checking appropriate to the language;\n- runtime validation at untrusted or cross-process boundaries;\n- unit and integration tests, plus browser, property, race, fuzz, or\n  concurrency tests where the risk model calls for them;\n- dependency vulnerability and supply-chain review;\n- a reproducible\n\n[truncated]",
