us browser, property, race, fuzz, or\n  concurrency tests where the risk model calls for them;\n- dependency vulnerability and supply-chain review;\n- a reproducible\n\n[truncated]"
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-1.json:162:              "content": "> **Source type:** Bounded current-project evidence pack synthesized from accepted Anvil decisions, repo state, Brain work history, and explicit user corrections\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Anvil Redux Current Orientation Evidence\n\n## Project identity and architecture\n\nAnvil Redux is Vik's personal Codex-first cognitive harness and the ecosystem\nthat makes Brain operational. It turns creative intent into excellent working\nsystems through Spark -> Shape -> Steel while preserving thought partnership,\ncontinuity, implementation discipline, verification, and adaptation.\n\nBrain is Rowan's maintained cognitive wiki. Anvil supplies host-neutral\ncapabilities with MCP as the default agent-facing operational adapter and CLI\nfor human/operator, diagnosis, and recovery paths. Continuity owns the compact\nhot working frame. Loci owns freshness-aware code navigation. llm-wiki owns the\nBrain wiki scaffold, page contract, graph/context tooling, lint, render, and\nwiki maintenance rules.\n\nCurrent user instruction and live repo evidence correct Brain when they\nconflict. Unqualified Anvil knowledge language means Brain unless a different\nstore is named. Operational journals and source archives remain evidence rather\nthan automatic Brain ingestion.\n\n## Current implementation doctrine\n\nVik explicitly corrected Anvil's quality target on 2026-07-18. Included\ncapabilities are designed and built on production-grade foundations from the\nstart. Incremental tasks, commits, and dogfood gates sequence and review the\nwork; they do not authorize minimum implementations or later-hardening debt.\nAnvil accepts complexity earned by the real production failure model while\nexcluding speculative features and unrelated scope.\n\nFor new projects and major subsystems, the accepted Gold-Class Language Tooling\nmodel routes Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, Rust, or another justified\nlanguage by workload. The profiles are revisable; platform-native exceptions\nand stable mixed-language boundaries remain valid.\n\n## Active work and shipped capabilities\n\n- Manifest is the accepted observational cross-project work substrate for\n  branching work, blockers, return points, evidence, Current, Frontier,\n  Chronicle, and Agent Context. It is separate from Continuity and remains\n  outside execution orchestration. Its corrected production-foundation plan is\n  at Vik's review gate. Storage is unresolved until a production qualification\n  compares JSONL, SQLite, or another local carrier.\n- Loci has shipped module-aware Go import resolution and stable package-node\n  targets. The current language-tooling return point is JavaScript/TypeScript\n  dependency-resolution completion design, followed by Cargo-aware Rust\n  support.\n- Brain bootstrap, prompt-specific recall, compact standing guidance, the\n  unified prompt hook, and the reversible native-memory cutover are implemented\n  and recorded in Brain work history.\n- Brain Steward handoff, the guarded maintenance-candidate loop, skill\n  invocation MCP evidence, Brain usage metrics, the dashboard, and its separate\n  Lens trace route are implemented.\n- Browser Eyes provides a local-only default renderer and an explicit\n  corporate CA-backed namespace. Browserless owns rendering while Anvil owns\n  policy, lifecycle, and MCP registration.\n- Brain Primitive coverage labels maintained pages for navigation and sparse\n  coverage investigation. Page count is not capability maturity, quality,\n  priority, or completeness.\n- Organic local conversation mining remains high-level design. Raw corpus and\n  speculative candidates cannot automatically enter Brain or mutate durable\n  systems.\n\n## Durable boundaries\n\n- Agent-facing operations default to bounded typed MCP adapters over\n  host-neutral cores. Hooks supply trusted provenance; skills guide workflows;\n  UIs provide human inspection without owning domain state.\n- Manifest owns durable work lifecycle and lineage. Continuity owns compact\n  o\n\n[truncated]"
docs/plans/evidence/brain-w23-independent-eval/review-1.json:215:              "content": "> **Source type:** Comparative research synthesis and explicit user approval\n> **Fetched:** 2026-07-18\n> **Do not edit this file. Edit the derived wiki page instead.**\n\n# Gold-Class Language Tooling Comparative Review And Approval\n\n## Approval\n\nAfter the first-pass language pages were marked provisional, Rowan completed\nand explained a broader comparative review to Vik. Vik approved the direction\nwith one explicit caveat: the model must be able to grow and change as Anvil\nlearns, projects produce evidence, and language ecosystems evolve.\n\nThis approval establishes a revisable operating model, not permanent stack\npolicy. The durable decision is how to reason about language choice and what\nquality every selected stack must provide. It does not freeze every framework,\npackage manager, linter, type checker, or runtime version.\n\nRelated Continuity decision: `evt_20260718_eca0a2950a7a`.\n\n## Evidence Posture\n\nThe review distinguished three kinds of support:\n\n1. **Official facts** establish language, runtime, and tool capabilities.\n2. **First-party production reports and surveys** show bounded experience in\n   real systems; they do not create universal rules.\n3. **Portfolio-routing conclusions** are Rowan and Vik's synthesis from those\n   facts, production cases, Anvil's needs, and explicit trade-offs.\n\nNo single popularity measure, benchmark, or company migration determines the\nright language for another workload.\n\n## Approved Portfolio Model\n\nPython, TypeScript, Go, and Rust are first-class capabilities for future Anvil\nwork. They are neither a ranking nor an exclusive list.\n\nStart from the dominant workload:\n\n| Workload | First language to evaluate | Main reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Browser-owned UI and web interaction | TypeScript | It is native to the JavaScript web platform and adds static authoring contracts. |\n| AI, ML, data, research, evaluation, and model orchestration | Python | Its domain ecosystem and iteration model are the dominant advantage. |\n| Network services, daemons, operational workers, and broadly distributed CLIs | Go | Its standard toolchain, concurrency model, and simple deployment usually minimise operational cost. |\n| Parsers, indexes, storage/native cores, embedded or constrained components, and strict latency/resource boundaries | Rust | Memory safety without a garbage collector and explicit resource control can satisfy requirements the simpler stacks cannot. |\n\nThese are starting points, not automatic answers. Use TypeScript for a Node\nservice when JavaScript ecosystem or shared web contracts dominate. Use Python\nfor a service when the AI/data boundary dominates. Use Rust for a service or\nCLI when measured latency, resource, safety, or native-integration requirements\njustify its additional implementation cost.\n\nUse native platform ecosystems when they own the product surface or required\ncapability: for example Swift on Apple platforms, Kotlin on Android, CUDA/C++\nfor NVIDIA-specific GPU work, or C# in an engine ecosystem that requires it.\nMCP is a protocol boundary, not a language choice; route an MCP implementation\nby the workload underneath it.\n\nMixed-language systems are valid when split at a stable, measurable boundary:\nfor example a Python AI host with a Rust native core, or a Go service shell\naround a Rust engine. Do not introduce a second language merely because it is\nindividually attractive.\n\n## Universal Gold-Class Quality Contract\n\nEvery long-lived project or major subsystem should provide:\n\n- a declared, pinned language/runtime and toolchain policy;\n- a manifest plus a committed lockfile where the ecosystem supports one;\n- deterministic format, static-check, test, and build commands;\n- strict static checking appropriate to the language;\n- runtime validation at untrusted or cross-process boundaries;\n- unit and integration tests, plus browser, property, race, fuzz, or\n  concurrency tests where the risk model calls for them;\n- dependency vulnerability and supply-chain review;\n- a reproducible\n\n[truncated]"
